            { "label": "Led to the 'Ruralisation' of India; increased the demographic burden on agriculture, accelerating poverty." },
            { "label": "Shifted India's status from a premier exporter of finished goods to a mere exporter of raw materials." },
            { "label": "**Debate**: Some revisionist historians argue that de-industrialization was not as severe as claimed, but the consensus remains that it was a major economic blight." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Commercialization of Agriculture",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Forced Crop Shifts",
          "children": [
            { "label": "British forced cultivation of cash crops for their industries: Indigo and Jute in Bengal, Cotton in Deccan, Opium in Bihar/UP, Tea in Assam." },
            { "label": "Tinkathia System in Champaran (Bihar) forced peasants to grow indigo on 3/20th of their best land." },
