{
  "id": "VIII",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "The 1857 Revolt",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Causes",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Political & Economic Causes",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Political: Dalhousie's aggressive Doctrine of Lapse (Satara, Jhansi, Nagpur); offensive annexation of Awadh on grounds of 'misgovernance' (1856); disrespect shown to Bahadur Shah Zafar (forced to vacate the Red Fort)." },
            { "label": "Economic: Destruction of traditional handicrafts by British imports; heavy taxation ruining peasants; Summary Settlement (1856) in Awadh that dispossessed many Taluqdars." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The political annexations alienated the ruling classes, while economic policies alienated peasants and artisans." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Social, Religious & Military Causes",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Social/Religious: Fear of mass conversion by Christian missionaries; abolition of Sati (1829) and the Widow Remarriage Act (1856) were seen as interference in Hindu customs." },
            { "label": "Religious Disabilities Act (1850): Allowed a Hindu convert to Christianity to inherit ancestral property, sparking fears of forced conversions." },
            { "label": "Military: General Service Enlistment Act (1856) forced sepoys to serve overseas, crossing the 'Kala Pani' (sea) which meant loss of caste; low pay, lack of prospects, and racial discrimination." },
            { "label": "Immediate Trigger: Introduction of the Enfield P-53 rifle with greased cartridges rumoured to be coated with cow and pig fat, offending both Hindus and Muslims." },
            { "label": "**Interlinked causes**: The revolt was a result of a confluence of political, economic, social, and military grievances." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Course of Events & Key Centres",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Outbreak",
          "children": [
            { "label": "First spark: Mangal Pandey (34th Native Infantry) attacked British officers at Barrackpore on 29 March 1857; he was hanged on 8 April." },
            { "label": "Actual outbreak: Meerut cantonment on 10 May 1857; sepoys mutinied, killed officers, and marched to Delhi the next day." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Meerut mutiny was spontaneous and quickly spread to Delhi, where the emperor was proclaimed the symbolic leader." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Leaders & Centres",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Delhi: Bahadur Shah Zafar as symbolic Emperor; military command under General Bakht Khan; British captured Delhi on 20 September 1857 under John Nicholson (died of wounds)." },
            { "label": "Kanpur: Nana Saheb (adopted son of Peshwa Baji Rao II) and his commander Tantia Tope; Satichaura Ghat massacre and Bibighar massacre; later recaptured by General Colin Campbell." },
            { "label": "Lucknow: Begum Hazrat Mahal led on behalf of her son Birjis Qadir; fierce resistance; finally evacuated after Campbell's second relief (March 1858)." },
            { "label": "Jhansi: Rani Laxmibai (1828–1858) joined the revolt after her adopted son Damodar Rao was denied the throne; died heroically fighting General Hugh Rose at the Battle of Gwalior (17 June 1858)." },
            { "label": "Bihar (Jagdishpur): Kunwar Singh (1777–1858), an 80‑year‑old zamindar, led a brilliant guerrilla campaign; died of wounds on 26 April 1858." },
            { "label": "Other Leaders: Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah of Faizabad (the 'Maulvi of the Revolt', executed June 1858); Khan Bahadur Khan (grandson of Hafiz Rahmat Khan) in Bareilly; Shah Mal (Jat peasant leader) in Baghpat (Pargana Baraut); Gonoo (Kol tribal leader) in Singhbhum." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The revolt was not confined to sepoys; it included peasants, zamindars, and rulers, making it a widespread uprising." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Nature, Failure & Consequences",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Historiography & Nature",
          "children": [
            { "label": "British historians (Sir John Lawrence, John Seeley, G.B.\nMalleson): Dismissed it as a mere 'Sepoy Mutiny' or a 'Muslim conspiracy'." },
            { "label": "V.D.\nSavarkar: Glorified it as 'The First War of Indian Independence' in his book published in 1909." },
            { "label": "R.C.\nMajumdar: Argued it was 'neither first, nor national, nor a war of independence'." },
            { "label": "Modern/Subaltern view: A peasant rebellion in uniform; highlighted by historians like Ranajit Guha." },
            { "label": "**Synthesis**: While not a unified national movement, the revolt was a significant and widespread challenge to British rule, with elements of anti-colonial sentiment." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Reasons for Failure",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Lack of a unified ideology, centralised leadership, and coordinated strategy." },
            { "label": "Geographically limited to North, Central, and Eastern India; South, West, and Punjab remained largely quiet or loyal." },
            { "label": "Lack of support: Educated middle class, major merchants, and most princely rulers (Scindia, Holkar, Nizam) actively aided the British; Sikhs and Gurkhas also supported the British." },
            { "label": "British superior resources: Better weaponry (Enfield rifles), telegraph for rapid communication, and unified command." },
            { "label": "**Lessons**: The failure highlighted the need for organized, pan-Indian, and ideologically coherent movements in the future." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Consequences",
          "children": [
            { "label": "End of EIC: Government of India Act (1858) transferred power to the British Crown; a Secretary of State for India replaced the Board of Control." },
            { "label": "Queen's Proclamation (1 November 1858): Read by Lord Canning at the Allahabad Durbar; promised an end to territorial annexations, respect for the rights of princes, non‑interference in religion, and equal opportunity (rarely honoured)." },
            { "label": "Army Reorganisation: Peel Commission (1859) recommended reducing Indian troops; ratio of British to Indian troops fixed at 1:2 in Bengal and 1:3 in Madras/Bombay; artillery kept exclusively with the British; recruitment based on the 'Martial Races' theory (Sikhs, Gurkhas, Pathans, Rajputs)." },
            { "label": "The Azamgarh Proclamation (25 August 1857): A rebel manifesto issued by the provisional government of Delhi, attributed to Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah or Firoz Shah; it appealed for Hindu‑Muslim unity and promised socio‑economic relief." },
            { "label": "**Long-term impact**: The revolt led to a fundamental restructuring of British rule, making it more centralized and cautious about social interference." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
