      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Bengal Associations",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Landholders' Society / Zamindari Association (1838): Founded by Dwarkanath Tagore; first organised political association, but focused solely on zamindar interests." },
            { "label": "Bengal British India Society (1843): Founded by George Thompson; aimed to discuss the grievances of the people." },
            { "label": "British India Association (1851): Merger of the Landholders' Society and Bengal British India Society; first truly all‑India association." },
            { "label": "Indian League (1875): Founded by Sisir Kumar Ghosh." },
            { "label": "Indian Association of Calcutta (1876): Founded by Surendranath Banerjea and Anandamohan Bose; the most important pre‑INC group; campaigned against the Vernacular Press Act (1878) and the reduction of ICS age limit (1877)." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These associations laid the groundwork for a pan-Indian political consciousness and the eventual formation of the INC." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Bombay, Madras & Abroad",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (1870): Founded by M.G.\nRanade and G.V.\nJoshi; served as a bridge between the government and the people." },
            { "label": "Bombay Presidency Association (1885): Founded by Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T.\nTelang, and Badruddin Tyabji." },
            { "label": "Madras Native Association (1852): First political organisation in Madras; later replaced by Madras Mahajan Sabha (1884) founded by M.\nViraraghavachari, G.\nSubramania Iyer, and P.\nAnandacharlu." },
            { "label": "East India Association (1866): Founded by Dadabhai Naoroji in London to influence British public opinion; London Indian Society (1865) was a precursor." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: These regional associations eventually coalesced to form the INC, representing the educated elite's desire for political representation." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Formation of INC (1885)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Foundation & Theories",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Formed largely through the initiative of Allan Octavian Hume, a retired British civil servant, with the backing of Lord Dufferin." },
            { "label": "Safety Valve Theory: Proposed by Lala Lajpat Rai; argued Hume formed the INC to release mounting Indian discontent peacefully, protecting the British empire from another 1857‑like explosion." },
            { "label": "Lightning Conductor Theory: Counter‑argument by G.K.\nGokhale; claimed early nationalists used Hume as a shield against immediate British repression." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The debate over the origins of the INC reflects differing views on whether it was a British tool or a genuine expression of Indian nationalism." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "The First Session",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Held at Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College, Bombay, from 28–31 December 1885 (originally planned for Pune, shifted due to cholera)." },
            { "label": "Presided over by Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee (W.C.\nBonnerjee); 72 delegates attended, mostly lawyers, journalists, and teachers." },
            { "label": "Adopted 9 resolutions: demanding a Royal Commission on Indian administration, expansion of legislative councils with elected members, holding simultaneous ICS exams in India and England, and reduction in military expenditure." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The INC began as a moderate, constitutional body, but it would evolve into the spearhead of the freedom struggle." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Moderate Phase (1885–1905)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Leaders & Ideology",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Key Leaders: Dadabhai Naoroji, G.K.\nGokhale, M.G.\nRanade, Surendranath Banerjea, Pherozeshah Mehta, Dinshaw Wacha, W.C.\nBonnerjee." },
            { "label": "Believed in the inherent justice and fairness of the British system; sought self‑government within the British Empire (Dominion Status), not complete independence." },
            { "label": "Methods: 3 P's – 'Prayer, Petition, and Persuasion'.\nStrictly constitutional agitation; avoided mass movements." },
            { "label": "**Critique**: The Moderate approach was criticized by later nationalists as 'political mendicancy', but it laid the foundation for political mobilization." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Achievements & Limitations",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Economic Critique: Their greatest achievement; through the 'Drain Theory' (Naoroji, R.C.\nDutt) they systematically proved British rule was the root cause of Indian poverty." },
            { "label": "Legislative Reform: Successfully pressured the government to pass the Indian Councils Act of 1892, which introduced indirect elections." },
            { "label": "Limitations: Confined to the urban educated elite ('macaulayputra'); lacked faith in the political capacity of the masses; dismissed by Extremists as 'political mendicancy' (begging)." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Moderates built the organizational infrastructure and ideological critique that the Extremists would later use to launch mass movements." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "X",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "Assertive Nationalism, Swadeshi & Home Rule (1905–1916)",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Partition of Bengal & Swadeshi Movement",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "The Partition (1905)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Announced by Lord Curzon on 19 July 1905; official reason: Bengal was too large (78.5 million people) to administer efficiently.\nReal motive: Divide and rule to break the nerve centre of Indian nationalism." },
            { "label": "Implemented on 16 October 1905; divided the Bengali‑speaking majority into East Bengal and Assam (Muslim majority) and West Bengal (Hindu majority)." },
            { "label": "Reaction: Observed as a day of mourning; Rabindranath Tagore composed 'Amar Sonar Bangla' and initiated the Rakhi Bandhan ceremony to symbolise unbreakable unity." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The partition was a clear example of 'divide and rule' and provoked a united response across Bengal." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "The Swadeshi Movement",
          "children": [
            { "label": "First major mass movement; moved beyond moderate methods.\nBoycott of foreign cloth, picketing of shops, and promotion of indigenous (Swadeshi) goods." },
            { "label": "National Education: National Council of Education (NCE) set up in March 1906; Bengal National College (August 1906) with Aurobindo Ghosh as its first principal." },
            { "label": "Role of Samitis: Swadesh Bandhab Samiti (Ashwini Kumar Dutta) mobilised masses in Barisal; widespread participation of students and women." },
            { "label": "Labour participation: First labour union, the Calcutta Printers' Union (1905), and strikes in foreign‑owned railways." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Swadeshi Movement marked the transition from moderate constitutionalism to mass agitation, and introduced boycott and swadeshi as enduring weapons." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Annulment",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Partition annulled on 12 December 1911 by Lord Hardinge II at the Delhi Durbar to curb revolutionary terrorism." },
            { "label": "Simultaneously, the capital was shifted from Calcutta to Delhi (announced 1911, shifted 1912) to reduce Bengal's political influence." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: Annulment was a tacit admission of the movement's success, but the capital shift was a strategic move to weaken Bengali nationalism." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Extremist Leadership & Surat Split (1907)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Rise of Extremism (Lal‑Bal‑Pal)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Lala Lajpat Rai (Punjab), Bal Gangadhar Tilak (Maharashtra), Bipin Chandra Pal (Bengal), and Aurobindo Ghosh." },
            { "label": "Tilak: Mobilised masses through Ganapati festival (1893) and Shivaji festival (1895).\nEdited Kesari (Marathi) and Mahratta (English).\nGave the slogan: 'Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it' (1905)." },
            { "label": "Aurobindo Ghosh: Propounded the theory of passive resistance in his newspaper 'Bande Mataram'." },
            { "label": "**Contrast with Moderates**: Extremists believed in direct action, boycotts, and self-reliance, while Moderates relied on petitions and gradual reform." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "The Surat Split (1907)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Moderates wanted to confine Swadeshi to Bengal; Extremists wanted to take it all‑India and include full boycott and non‑cooperation." },
            { "label": "At the 1907 Surat Session (presided by Rash Behari Ghosh), physical clashes occurred between the two factions; Congress split, severely weakening the national movement." },
            { "label": "British immediately repressed the leaderless Extremists: Tilak sentenced to 6 years' transportation to Mandalay (1908–14); Aurobindo retired to Pondicherry (1910); severe press acts (1908, 1910) were passed." },
            { "label": "**Assessment**: The split was a setback, but it also allowed the Extremists to develop their own ideological base and later rejoin the Congress in 1916." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Home Rule Movement & Lucknow Pact",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Home Rule League Movement (1915–16)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Inspired by the Irish Home Rule movement; the demand was self‑government within the British Empire, not complete independence." },
            { "label": "Tilak's League: Formed on 28 April 1916 at Belgaum; operated in Maharashtra (except Bombay city), Karnataka, Central Provinces, and Berar; had 6 branches." },
            { "label": "Annie Besant's League: Formed on 1 September 1916 at Madras; operated in the rest of India including Bombay city; had about 200 branches and a much wider reach." },
            { "label": "Significance: Kept the political atmosphere alive during WWI; bridged the gap between Extremist and upcoming Gandhian mass politics." },
            { "label": "**Impact**: The Home Rule Movement demonstrated the viability of mass mobilization and prepared the ground for Gandhi's entry." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Lucknow Pact (1916)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Historical session presided by A.C.\nMajumdar; Extremists were readmitted into the Congress, reunifying the party." },
            { "label": "Congress and Muslim League signed the Lucknow Pact, presenting joint constitutional demands: separate electorates for Muslims, elected majorities in councils, and half the Viceroy's Executive Council to be Indians." },
            { "label": "Controversy: Congress officially accepted the Muslim League's demand for separate electorates, which historians argue validated communal politics as a permanent feature." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The Lucknow Pact is seen as a high-water mark of Hindu-Muslim unity, but also as a concession that later deepened communal divisions." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XI",
  "p": "ps",
  "label": "Revolutionary Nationalism",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Bengal Revolutionary Movement (Pre‑WWI)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Early Samitis & Groups",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Anushilan Samiti (1902): Founded in Calcutta by Promotha Mitter, Aurobindo Ghose, and Barindra Kumar Ghose; first secret revolutionary society." },
            { "label": "Jugantar (1906): Inner circle of Anushilan; founded by Bhupendranath Datta and Barin Ghose; published the revolutionary weekly 'Jugantar'." },
            { "label": "India House (1905): Established by Shyamji Krishna Varma in London; a hub for Indian revolutionaries abroad; journal 'Indian Sociologist'." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These groups pioneered the use of armed resistance and bomb-making, inspiring later revolutionaries." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Conspiracies",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Muzaffarpur Conspiracy (30 April 1908): Khudiram Bose and Prafulla Chaki threw a bomb at a carriage meant for the unpopular Judge Kingsford; two innocent British women were killed.\nChaki shot himself; Bose was hanged on 11 August 1908 (aged 18)." },
            { "label": "Alipore Bomb Case (1908–09): An illegal arms factory was discovered at Maniktala, Calcutta.\nAurobindo Ghosh was tried but acquitted (defended brilliantly by C.R.\nDas); Barindra Ghosh was sentenced to the Andamans." },
            { "label": "Barrah Dacoity (2 June 1908): Organised by the Dhaka Anushilan Samiti (Pulin Behari Das, Prafulla Chakraborty) to raise funds for revolutionary activities." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: These events demonstrated the shift from moderate protest to revolutionary violence, which the British responded to with repressive laws." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Maharashtra & Abroad Activities",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Maharashtra Revolutionaries",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Chapekar Brothers (Damodar and Balkrishna): Assassinated W.C.\nRand (Plague Commissioner of Pune) on 22 June 1897 for his oppressive measures during the plague epidemic." },
            { "label": "Abhinav Bharat Society (1904): A secret society founded by V.D.\nSavarkar, inspired by Mazzini's 'Young Italy'." },
            { "label": "Nasik Conspiracy Case (21 December 1909): A.M.T.\nJackson (Collector of Nasik) was assassinated by Anant Laxman Kanhere (a member of Abhinav Bharat); Savarkar was arrested and sentenced to two life terms (50 years) in the Andamans." },
            { "label": "Delhi Conspiracy Case (23 December 1912): Rashbehari Bose and Basant Kumar Biswas threw a bomb at Lord Hardinge II during his ceremonial entry into Delhi; Hardinge was wounded, but the revolutionaries escaped (Bose later fled to Japan)." },
