            { "label": "Published the weekly 'Ghadar' (Rebellion) newspaper in Urdu, Hindi, Punjabi, and Marathi; the masthead read 'Enemy of the British Government'." },
            { "label": "Komagata Maru incident (1914): A Japanese ship carrying 376 Indian immigrants (mostly Sikh) was denied entry into Canada and forced to return to Budge Budge (Calcutta) where police firing killed 22; this inflamed anti‑British passions." },
            { "label": "Attempted a pan‑Indian armed mutiny in February 1915, which was betrayed by a spy (Kirpal Singh); ruthlessly crushed via the Defence of India Act 1915." },
            { "label": "**Assessment**: The Ghadar movement was a significant transnational revolutionary effort, though it failed due to lack of coordination." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "HRA & HSRA (1924–31)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Hindustan Republican Association (HRA)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Founded in October 1924 at Kanpur by Sachindra Nath Sanyal (author of 'Bandi Jeevan'), Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee, and Ramprasad Bismil." },
            { "label": "Kakori Train Robbery (9 August 1925): HRA looted the British treasury from the 8 Down train near Kakori (Lucknow).\nResulted in the trial and hanging of Ramprasad Bismil, Ashfaqullah Khan, Roshan Singh, and Rajendra Lahiri on 19 December 1927." },
