{
  "id": "XII",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "Gandhi's Entry & Local Experiments (1915–1919)",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Gandhi's Framework & Ideology",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Core Philosophy",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Returned to India from South Africa on 9 January 1915 (Pravasi Bharatiya Divas).\nFirst major public appearance: opening of Banaras Hindu University (Feb 1916)." },
            { "label": "Hind Swaraj (1909): Written aboard SS Kildonan Castle; offered a scathing critique of modern Western, industrial civilisation and advocated village‑centric self‑rule." },
            { "label": "Satyagraha (Truth‑force): His political weapon, involving active, non‑violent resistance and self‑suffering to change the opponent's heart.\nFirst practiced in South Africa at the Empire Theatre protest (1906)." },
            { "label": "Trusteeship: Economic theory stating capitalists should not own wealth absolutely but hold it in 'trust' for the welfare of workers; later elaborated in his Harijan articles." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Gandhi's ideology was a synthesis of Indian spirituality and Western political thought, creating a unique approach to anti-colonial struggle." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Early Local Satyagrahas (CAKe)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Champaran Satyagraha (1917) – First Civil Disobedience",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Invited by Raj Kumar Shukla, a poor indigo cultivator, to Champaran (Bihar) to investigate the Tinkathia system, where European planters forced peasants to grow indigo on 3/20th of their best land." },
            { "label": "Defied the district magistrate's order to leave; joined by Rajendra Prasad, J.B.\nKripalani, Mazhar‑ul‑Haq, Mahadev Desai, Narhari Parikh." },
            { "label": "Result: Government formed an Enquiry Committee (Gandhi was a member) leading to the Champaran Agrarian Act (1918), which abolished Tinkathia and provided 25% compensation to peasants." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Champaran was Gandhi's first successful application of satyagraha in India and established his leadership." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918) – First Hunger Strike",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Dispute between textile mill owners (led by Ambalal Sarabhai) and workers over the withdrawal of the 'Plague Bonus' (introduced during the plague epidemic)." },
            { "label": "Workers demanded 50% wage hike; owners offered 20%.\nGandhi intervened, advised a compromise of 35%, and undertook a fast unto death (his first public fast) to rally the wavering workers." },
            { "label": "Result: A tribunal was set up which awarded a 35% wage increase.\nAnasuya Sarabhai (sister of Ambalal) led the workers' side." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The strike demonstrated Gandhi's use of fasting as a political tool and his ability to mediate between labour and capital." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Kheda Satyagraha (1918) – First Non‑Cooperation",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Gujarat peasants faced crop failure; according to the Revenue Code, if the yield was under one‑fourth of the normal produce, revenue should be remitted, but the government refused." },
            { "label": "Gandhi and Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel urged peasants to withhold revenue payments.\nIndulal Yagnik was a key organiser." },
            { "label": "Result: Government secretly instructed officials to recover revenue only from those who could afford to pay; the campaign gave Patel his first mass leadership experience." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Kheda showed that satyagraha could work at the village level and that peasants could be mobilized for a just cause." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Rowlatt Act & Jallianwala Bagh (1919)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Rowlatt Act & Satyagraha",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Official name: Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act (March 1919).\nPassed despite the united opposition of all Indian members of the Imperial Legislative Council." },
            { "label": "Extended draconian wartime Defence of India Act powers into peacetime; allowed arrest without warrant, detention without trial for up to 2 years, and trial in camera without a jury." },
            { "label": "Slogan: 'No vakil, no daleel, no appeal' – termed the 'Black Act'.\nGandhi formed the Satyagraha Sabha in Bombay and called for a nationwide Hartal on 6 April 1919." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Rowlatt Act was a catalyst for the mass movement; it united diverse sections against a common repressive measure." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April 1919)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "A peaceful crowd of about 20,000 gathered in the enclosed Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar, on Baisakhi day to protest the arrest of local leaders Dr.\nSaifuddin Kitchlew and Dr.\nSatyapal." },
            { "label": "Brigadier‑General Reginald Dyer blocked the only exit and ordered Gurkha and Baluchi troops to fire; 1,650 rounds were shot for about 10 minutes, killing nearly 1,000 people and wounding over 1,200 (official Hunter Commission figures)." },
            { "label": "Reactions: Rabindranath Tagore renounced his knighthood; Gandhi returned his Kaiser‑i‑Hind medal; Sir Shankaran Nair resigned from the Viceroy's Executive Council; S.N.\nBanerjea returned his title." },
            { "label": "Hunter Committee (formed Oct 1919, report March 1920): An official inquiry that condemned Dyer's actions as 'inhuman and un‑British' but imposed no penal action.\nDyer was relieved of command but hailed as a hero by the British public and presented with a sword and a purse of £26,000." },
            { "label": "Shocked by the escalating violence (post‑office burning, railway sabotage), Gandhi withdrew the Rowlatt Satyagraha on 18 April 1919, terming it a 'Himalayan Blunder' to launch a mass movement before the people were adequately trained in non‑violence." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Jallianwala Bagh was a turning point, permanently alienating Indians from British rule and leading to Gandhi's decision to launch non-cooperation." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XIII",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "Khilafat & Non‑Cooperation Movement (1919–1922)",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Khilafat Movement",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Background & Formation",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Post‑WWI, the British dismembered the Ottoman Empire and the Treaty of Sèvres (1920) stripped the Sultan of Turkey of his religious authority as Caliph (Khalifa)." },
            { "label": "Indian Muslims formed the All India Khilafat Committee in November 1919 led by the Ali Brothers (Muhammad Ali and Shaukat Ali), Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, Hakim Ajmal Khan, and Hasrat Mohani.\nThe Khilafat Day was observed on 17 October 1919." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Khilafat issue was a trans-national religious concern that galvanized Indian Muslims and created an opportunity for unity with the Congress." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Alliance with Congress",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Gandhi saw this as a once‑in‑a‑century opportunity to cement Hindu‑Muslim unity and merged the Khilafat cause with the demand for Swaraj." },
            { "label": "Decline: The movement lost relevance when Mustafa Kemal Atatürk seized power in Turkey, abolished the Sultanate (1922), and formally abolished the Caliphate on 3 March 1924." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Khilafat alliance demonstrated that religious issues could be used to build national unity, but its decline also exposed the fragility of such alliances." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Non‑Cooperation Movement (1920–22)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Adoption & Programme",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Special Session in Calcutta (4–9 September 1920, presided by Lala Lajpat Rai) approved NCM; Nagpur Session (December 1920, presided by C.\nVijayaraghavachariar) ratified it, restructured Congress on a linguistic basis, and lowered the membership fee to 4 annas.\nThe tricolour flag was adopted." },
            { "label": "Negative Programme: Boycott of government schools, colleges, law courts, legislative councils, foreign cloth, and surrender of titles and honours." },
            { "label": "Constructive Programme: Promotion of khadi, establishment of national schools (Jamia Millia Islamia founded 1920, shifted to Delhi; Kashi Vidyapeeth 1921; Gujarat Vidyapeeth 1920), Hindu‑Muslim unity, removal of untouchability." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The programme was comprehensive, aiming to create self-reliance and moral strength." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Mass Participation",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Massive student dropouts; top lawyers like Motilal Nehru, C.R.\nDas, C.\nRajagopalachari, and Sardar Patel gave up their lucrative practices." },
            { "label": "Peasants linked their local struggles (Awadh Kisan Sabha, Eka Movement, Moplah Rebellion 1921) to the national movement." },
            { "label": "The Tilak Swaraj Fund, set up to finance the movement, was massively oversubscribed – the target of Rs.\n1 crore was collected within a few months (actually exceeded)." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: This was the first truly mass movement, mobilizing students, lawyers, peasants, and workers." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Chauri Chaura & Withdrawal",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Chauri Chaura incident (5 February 1922): A mob of about 3,000 peasants in Gorakhpur district, UP, set fire to a police station, burning 22 policemen alive (some sources say 23)." },
            { "label": "A deeply disturbed Gandhi abruptly withdrew the entire NCM via the Bardoli Resolution of the Congress Working Committee on 12 February 1922." },
            { "label": "Young leaders like J.L.\nNehru and Subhas Chandra Bose, along with veterans like C.R.\nDas, heavily criticized this unilateral withdrawal.\nGandhi was arrested on 10 March 1922 and sentenced to 6 years' imprisonment (released in Feb 1924 on health grounds)." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The withdrawal is debated – some see it as a wise move to prevent further violence, others as a betrayal of the masses." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XIV",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "Civil Disobedience Movement (1930–1934)",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Lead‑up to the Movement",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Swarajists & Simon Commission",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Swaraj Party (1 January 1923): Formed by C.R.\nDas (President) and Motilal Nehru (Secretary).\nAimed to enter councils to 'deadlock' the government ('responsive cooperation').\nWon 42 out of 141 seats in the Central Legislative Assembly in 1923; declined after Das's death on 16 June 1925." },
            { "label": "Simon Commission (appointed Nov 1927, arrived 3 Feb 1928): An all‑white, 7‑member commission under Sir John Simon sent to review the 1919 Act.\nBoycotted with the slogan 'Simon Go Back'.\nLala Lajpat Rai died on 17 November 1928 from injuries sustained in a police lathi charge while leading a protest in Lahore." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Swarajists' council entry was a tactical move, but the Simon Commission boycott re-ignited mass unity." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Nehru Report & Jinnah's Counter",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Nehru Report (August 1928): Drafted by a committee chaired by Motilal Nehru.\nDemanded Dominion Status, joint electorates with reserved seats for minorities, 19 fundamental rights, and a federal structure with residuary powers at the Centre.\nRejected by the Muslim League (denied separate electorates) and by younger Congress radicals who demanded Purna Swaraj." },
            { "label": "Jinnah's 14 Points (28 March 1929): A counter‑proposal demanding a federal structure with residuary powers to provinces, 1/3rd Muslim representation at the Centre, separate electorates, and safeguards for Muslim culture and religion." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These constitutional proposals highlighted the growing divide between Congress and the League, setting the stage for future communalism." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Lahore Congress & Gandhi's Demands",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Lahore Session (29–31 December 1929): J.L.\nNehru as president.\nThe Congress declared Purna Swaraj (Complete Independence) as its goal.\nOn 26 January 1930, the Declaration of Independence was publicly read, and the first Independence Day was celebrated." },
            { "label": "Gandhi issued an ultimatum of 11 Demands to Lord Irwin on 31 January 1930, prominently including the abolition of the salt tax, 50% reduction in land revenue, reduction in military spending, and release of political prisoners.\nIrwin ignored it." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Purna Swaraj declaration marked a shift from Dominion Status to complete independence, galvanizing the masses." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "The Movement & Salt March",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "The Dandi March (12 March – 6 April 1930)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Gandhi chose salt as the central symbol because it affected every class equally, bypassing communal divides." },
            { "label": "Started from Sabarmati Ashram with 78 handpicked followers; walked 241 miles (390 km) over 24 days to the coastal village of Dandi." },
            { "label": "Broke the salt law by boiling seawater at 6:30 AM on 6 April 1930, officially launching the Civil Disobedience Movement.\nSimultaneously, salt laws were broken across India." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Dandi March was a masterstroke of symbolism, turning a mundane issue into a national movement." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Regional Spread & Mass Action",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Tamil Nadu: C.\nRajagopalachari led the Salt March from Tiruchirappalli to Vedaranyam (April–May 1930)." },
            { "label": "Malabar (Kerala): K.\nKelappan (the 'Kerala Gandhi') led the march to Payyanur." },
            { "label": "Dharasana Satyagraha (21 May 1930): After Gandhi's arrest, Sarojini Naidu, Imam Saheb, and Manilal Gandhi led a massive non‑violent raid on the Dharasana salt works; brutal police beatings were reported globally by American journalist Webb Miller, turning world opinion against British rule." },
            { "label": "NWFP: Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (Frontier Gandhi) led the 'Khudai Khidmatgars' (Red Shirts), a non‑violent army; the Garhwal Rifles famously refused to fire on unarmed Pathan protesters at Peshawar (23 April 1930)." },
            { "label": "Northeast: 13‑year‑old Naga spiritual leader Rani Gaidinliu led a revolt against the British; captured and jailed until 1947." },
            { "label": "Sholapur (Maharashtra): Mill workers established a virtual parallel government for about a week (May 1930)." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The movement was truly pan-Indian, with significant participation from women, peasants, and workers." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Round Table Conferences & Pacts",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "First RTC & Gandhi‑Irwin Pact",
          "children": [
            { "label": "First Round Table Conference (12 November 1930 – 19 January 1931): Held in London.\nCongress boycotted it; most leaders were in jail.\nThe Conference was attended by the Muslim League, princely states, and others; it failed to produce any agreement." },
            { "label": "Gandhi‑Irwin Pact (Delhi Pact, 5 March 1931): Irwin agreed to release all non‑violent political prisoners, permit the manufacture of salt for personal use, and allow peaceful picketing.\nGandhi agreed to suspend the CDM and attend the Second RTC." },
            { "label": "Karachi Session (29 March 1931): Presided by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.\nEndorsed the Gandhi‑Irwin Pact; passed historic resolutions on Fundamental Rights and National Economic Programme (drafted by J.L.\nNehru), including guarantees of freedom of speech, association, and the right to work." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The Karachi resolutions became a blueprint for the later constitutional framework of independent India." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Second & Third RTCs",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Second RTC (7 September – 1 December 1931): Gandhi attended as the sole Congress representative.\nFailed entirely due to the united front of minorities (Muslims, Dalits, Sikhs, etc.) demanding separate electorates, which Gandhi strongly opposed." },
            { "label": "Third RTC (17 November – 24 December 1932): Congress did not attend; attended by only a few loyalists and the League.\nThe discussions were academic and ineffective." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The failure of the RTCs demonstrated the British strategy of playing minorities against the Congress, and the deep communal divisions." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Communal Award & Poona Pact",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Communal Award (16 August 1932): British PM Ramsay MacDonald announced separate electorates for the Depressed Classes (Dalits) in addition to the existing separate electorates for Muslims, Sikhs, etc." },
            { "label": "Gandhi began a fast‑unto‑death in Yerawada Jail on 20 September 1932.\nThis led to the Poona Pact (24 September 1932) between Gandhi (represented by Madan Mohan Malaviya) and Dr.\nB.R.\nAmbedkar." },
            { "label": "Result: Separate electorates for Dalits were abandoned in favour of joint electorates, but the number of reserved seats for Depressed Classes was increased from 71 to 147 in provincial legislatures and to 18% at the Centre." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The Poona Pact is seen as a compromise; it prevented the division of the Hindu vote but also maintained caste-based reservations, which Ambedkar later regretted." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XV",
  "p": "pm3",
  "label": "Quit India Movement (1942)",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Context & Outbreak",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Individual Satyagraha (1940–41)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Launched by Gandhi in response to the rejection of the August Offer (1940); a limited, symbolic protest to affirm the right to free speech against the war." },
            { "label": "First Satyagrahi: Vinoba Bhave (17 October 1940); Second: J.L.\nNehru; Third: Brahma Datt.\nOver 25,000 individual satyagrahis courted arrest." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: This prepared the ground for the larger Quit India movement by asserting the right to oppose the war." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Cripps Mission (March–April 1942)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Sir Stafford Cripps sent to India by the British War Cabinet to secure Indian support for the war effort in the wake of Japanese advances in Southeast Asia." },
            { "label": "Offered Dominion Status after the war, an Indian constitution‑making body, but included a 'provincial option' clause: any province could refuse to join the new Union – a blueprint for partition." },
            { "label": "Rejected by Congress (wanted immediate power transfer and control of defence) and Muslim League (wanted explicit Pakistan).\nGandhi called it a 'post‑dated cheque on a crashing bank'." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Cripps Mission's failure made it clear that the British would not concede real power, leading to the Quit India demand." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "The August Revolution",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Gowalia Tank Maidan, Bombay (8 August 1942): The All India Congress Committee ratified the Quit India Resolution.\nGandhi delivered the famous mantra: 'Do or Die' (Karenge ya Marenge)." },
            { "label": "Operation Zero Hour: In the early morning of 9 August 1942, the British arrested the entire top Congress leadership (Gandhi, Nehru, Azad, Patel, etc.) and the party was declared illegal, leaving the movement leaderless." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Despite the arrests, the movement spread spontaneously across the country, showing the depth of nationalist sentiment." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm3",
      "label": "Nature of QIM & Parallel Governments",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "A Spontaneous Mass Uprising",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Without central leadership, the masses took charge.\nHighly violent compared to previous movements – telegraph wires cut, railway tracks uprooted, post offices burnt, police stations attacked." },
            { "label": "Underground network: Young leaders like Jayaprakash Narayan, Ram Manohar Lohia, Aruna Asaf Ali, and Achyut Patwardhan directed activities.\nAruna Asaf Ali hoisted the national flag at Gowalia Tank." },
            { "label": "Usha Mehta ran the secret underground 'Congress Radio' (Voice of Freedom), broadcasting news and instructions from an unknown location." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The movement was a true mass revolt, but its lack of central coordination made it easier for the British to suppress." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Parallel Governments",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Ballia (UP): First parallel government established by Chittu Pandey in August 1942; lasted about a week before being crushed." },
            { "label": "Tamluk (Bengal): The 'Jatiya Sarkar' under Satish Chandra Samanta set up Vidyut Vahinis (armed squads), a police force, and undertook cyclone relief work; lasted until September 1944." },
            { "label": "Satara (Maharashtra): The 'Prati Sarkar' led by Nana Patil and others; lasted the longest (1942–1945), set up Nyayadan Mandals (people's courts) and revenue collection." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These parallel governments demonstrated that the Indian people were ready to govern themselves, even in the absence of formal power." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Significance & British Response",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Impact on National Movement",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Permanently shifted the political paradigm: anything short of immediate, complete independence was no longer acceptable." },
            { "label": "Proved conclusively to the British that they could no longer govern India without the active consent of the Indians." },
            { "label": "Communist Party of India (CPI) boycotted QIM due to their 'People's War' line (supporting the USSR and Britain against Fascism); the Muslim League also opposed it, calling for a 'Divide and Quit'." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The Quit India Movement is hailed as the final mass campaign that forced the British to quit India." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "British Repression",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Most brutal state repression since 1857.\nOver 100,000 arrested, entire villages fined, and aerial strafing used against crowds.\nUnofficial estimates suggest 7,000–10,000 people were killed." },
            { "label": "Despite the violent suppression, the movement made the British realise that their time in India was limited." },
            { "label": "**Assessment**: The British response showed that they were willing to use extreme force to maintain control, but it also strengthened the resolve of the nationalists." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XVI",
  "p": "pm2",
  "label": "Subhas Chandra Bose & Indian National Army",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Subhas Chandra Bose",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Congress Presidency & Tripuri Crisis",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Unanimously elected Congress President at the Haripura Session (February 1938); set up the National Planning Committee (October 1938) under J.L.\nNehru, with himself as chairman, to plan for industrialisation." },
            { "label": "Tripuri Crisis (March 1939): Bose stood for re‑election against Pattabhi Sitaramayya (Gandhi's candidate) and won by 1,580 to 1,375 votes.\nGandhi declared it 'my own defeat'." },
            { "label": "Due to lack of cooperation from the Congress Working Committee, Bose resigned in April 1939 and formed the Forward Bloc (3 May 1939) as a left‑wing faction within the Congress." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The Tripuri crisis highlighted the ideological rift between Gandhi's non-violent approach and Bose's militant, radical vision." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Escape & Global Alliances",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Placed under house arrest in Calcutta (July 1940); dramatically escaped in disguise on 17 January 1941, travelling via Peshawar, Kabul, Moscow (with Soviet help), and finally reaching Berlin (March 1941)." },
            { "label": "Met Hitler in May 1942; set up the Free India Centre in Berlin and broadcast anti‑British propaganda over the Azad Hind Radio.\nIn February 1943, he left Germany aboard the German submarine U‑180, transferring to the Japanese submarine I‑29 off Madagascar, and reached Singapore in July 1943." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: Bose's international networking demonstrated his strategic thinking and willingness to use Axis powers to achieve India's freedom." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "Indian National Army (Azad Hind Fauj)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Formation",
          "children": [
            { "label": "The INA was first conceptualised by Captain Mohan Singh in Malaya (1942) from Indian POWs surrendered by the British; however, it was disbanded after differences with the Japanese." },
            { "label": "Bose arrived in Singapore (2 July 1943), completely reorganised the INA, and on 21 October 1943 proclaimed the Provisional Government of Free India (Azad Hind), recognised by the Axis powers and Japan.\nIts headquarters were in Singapore and later Rangoon." },
            { "label": "Created a dedicated women's combat unit, the Rani of Jhansi Regiment, led by Captain Lakshmi Sahgal (later Minister for Women's Affairs in the Azad Hind government)." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The INA was a significant military force that posed a direct challenge to British rule, even if ultimately unsuccessful." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Military Campaigns",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Rallying cries: 'Jai Hind', 'Delhi Chalo', and 'Give me blood, and I shall give you freedom' (spoken at a rally in Singapore)." },
            { "label": "The INA (about 40,000 soldiers) participated in the Japanese offensive in Burma and India (Imphal and Kohima, March–June 1944).\nThe tricolour was hoisted in Moirang, Manipur, on 14 April 1944." },
            { "label": "Campaign failed due to early monsoons, logistical collapse, Allied counter‑offensive, and Japanese retreat.\nBose is believed to have died in a plane crash in Taipei on 18 August 1945, though controversy persists." },
            { "label": "**Assessment**: Despite military failure, the INA's actions inspired a wave of anti-British sentiment and were a major factor in the RIN mutiny." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "pm2",
      "label": "INA Trials & RIN Mutiny",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "The Red Fort Trials (November 1945 – May 1946)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "The British held public courts‑martial at the Red Fort, Delhi, for three INA officers: Colonel Prem Kumar Sahgal, Colonel Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon, and Major General Shah Nawaz Khan – representing a Hindu, a Sikh, and a Muslim." },
            { "label": "The trial sparked an unprecedented nationwide upsurge.\nThe INA Defence Committee was formed, including legal heavyweights like Bhulabhai Desai (lead counsel), Tej Bahadur Sapru, J.L.\nNehru (who donned his lawyer's robe after 25 years), and K.N.\nKatju." },
            { "label": "Though convicted, the three officers were released due to public pressure, demonstrating the British fear of a nationalist revolt in the armed forces." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The trials turned the INA men into national heroes and eroded British confidence in the loyalty of the Indian military." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Royal Indian Navy (RIN) Mutiny (18–23 February 1946)",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Naval ratings on HMIS Talwar in Bombay (Karachi too) went on strike protesting terrible food, racial discrimination, and demanding the release of INA prisoners." },
            { "label": "Spread to 78 ships and 20 shore establishments, involving 20,000 ratings.\nThe mutineers hoisted the flags of the Congress, Muslim League, and Communist Party together on the ships." },
            { "label": "Sardar Patel and M.A.\nJinnah intervened and persuaded the ratings to surrender on 23 February 1946 to prevent uncontrolled bloodshed.\nThe strike committee was led by M.S.\nKhan." },
            { "label": "Significance: The final nail in the coffin of the British Empire; the British realised that their ultimate instrument of control – the armed forces – could no longer be trusted." },
            { "label": "**Historiography**: The RIN mutiny is often seen as the decisive event that convinced the British to quit India." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XVII",
  "p": "ps",
  "label": "Socialists, Communists & Left Movements",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Congress Socialist Party (CSP)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Formation & Ideology",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Formed in October 1934 at a meeting in Bombay, within the Indian National Congress, by young leaders disillusioned with the suspension of the CDM and the retreat after 1931." },
            { "label": "Founders: Jayaprakash Narayan, Acharya Narendra Deva, Minoo Masani, Yusuf Meherally.\nJ.P.\nNarayan became its first General Secretary." },
            { "label": "Aim: To shape Congress ideology towards democratic socialism, radical agrarian reforms, and mobilise peasant and worker bases.\nIt remained a pressure group within the Congress until 1948 when the Congress barred dual membership." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The CSP brought socialist ideas into the mainstream Congress, influencing the party's leftward shift." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Communist Party of India (CPI)",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Formation & Early Conspiracies",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Originally founded in Tashkent on 17 October 1920 by M.N.\nRoy, Abani Mukherji, and others as the émigré Communist Party of India.\nM.N.\nRoy was the first to propose a communist party for India." },
            { "label": "Formally structured within India at the Kanpur Bolshevik Conspiracy Case (1924), where S.A.\nDange, Muzaffar Ahmad, Shaukat Usmani, and Nalini Gupta were tried." },
            { "label": "Meerut Conspiracy Case (March 1929 – January 1933): 33 prominent labour and communist leaders (including three Englishmen – Philip Spratt, Ben Bradley, and Lester Hutchinson) were arrested for conspiring to overthrow British rule.\nThe trial brought immense national sympathy to the communist cause." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The CPI's early history was marked by state repression, which ironically helped legitimize their cause." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Later Phase",
          "children": [
            { "label": "People's War Thesis (December 1941): After Hitler invaded the USSR, the CPI dramatically shifted from opposing the British to supporting the Allied war effort, calling the war a 'People's War'.\nThis led them to oppose the Quit India Movement (1942), causing lasting friction with nationalists." },
            { "label": "P.C.\nJoshi was the General Secretary during the 1930s–40s, significantly expanding mass membership and establishing the cultural wing: Indian People's Theatre Association (IPTA) in 1943, which used art as propaganda." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: The CPI's shift during WWII alienated them from the mainstream, but they later gained influence in post-independence politics." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Trade Union Movement",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "AITUC & Splits",
          "children": [
            { "label": "All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC) founded on 31 October 1920 in Bombay; Lala Lajpat Rai was its first President and Diwan Chaman Lall the General Secretary." },
            { "label": "Organised the famous Bombay Textile Strike (April–October 1928) led by the communist Girni Kamgar Union; it was a massive strike involving 150,000 workers." },
            { "label": "First split in 1929: Moderates (N.M.\nJoshi, V.V.\nGiri) left to form the Indian Trade Union Federation (ITUF).\nIn 1931, communists formed the Red Trade Union Congress (RTUC), but the two merged back into AITUC in 1935." },
            { "label": "In May 1947, Congress‑aligned leaders formed the Indian National Trade Union Congress (INTUC) to counter communist dominance, with Sardar Patel's support." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: The splits in the trade union movement reflected the broader ideological divisions in the freedom struggle." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Factory Acts & Early Labour Leaders",
          "children": [
            { "label": "N.M.\nJoshi founded the Social Service League (1911) in Bombay." },
            { "label": "First Factory Act (1881): Passed by Lord Ripon based on the recommendations of the Bombay Factory Commission (1875); banned employment of children under 7, regulated hours for children 7–12 (9 hours/day), and provided for four holidays a month." },
            { "label": "Second Factory Act (1891): Extended regulation to all factories employing more than 50 workers; regulated women's working hours (11 hours/day with a 1.5‑hour rest); prohibited women from night work." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These acts were early attempts to regulate labour conditions, though they were often inadequately enforced." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
},
{
  "id": "XVIII",
  "p": "ps",
  "label": "Peasant, Tribal & Worker Movements",
  "children": [
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Tribal Movements",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "Pre-1857 Uprisings",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Pahariya Rebellion (1778): Rajmahal Hills; British forced to pacify them by declaring 'Daman-i-Koh' as forbidden/protected territory." },
            { "label": "Chuar Uprising (1798-99): Led by Durjan Singh in the Jungle Mahal region against oppressive land revenue." },
            { "label": "Khasi Revolt (1829-33): Led by Tirath Singh in Meghalaya against British road construction through their hills; U Tirot Sing later led a more famous phase." },
            { "label": "Kol Uprising (1831-32): Led by Buddha Bhagat in Chotanagpur; triggered by massive land transfers from tribal headmen to outsider Sikh/Muslim thikadars." },
            { "label": "Pagal Panthis (1825-33): Led by Karam Shah and Tipu Shah in Mymensingh (Bengal); a syncretic religious and political movement against zamindars and British." },
            { "label": "Santhal Hul (1855-56): Led by brothers Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu against Dikus (outsiders) and moneylenders.\nDeclared independence on June 30, 1855 (Hul Diwas); violently suppressed." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: These early tribal revolts were responses to land alienation and colonial oppression, often led by charismatic leaders." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Post-1857 Uprisings",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Munda Ulgulan (Great Tumult, 1899-1900): Led by Birsa Munda in Ranchi against the destruction of the Khuntkatti (joint-holding) system and forced labour.\nBirsa died in jail (June 1900).\nLed to the Chotanagpur Tenancy Act (1908)." },
            { "label": "Bhil Revolts: Led by Govind Guru; culminated in the tragic Mangarh Massacre (17 November 1913) where about 1,500 tribals were gunned down by British forces." },
            { "label": "Tana Bhagat Movement (1914): Initiated by Jatra Bhagat in Chotanagpur; a Sanskritization movement that later adopted Gandhian non-violence and participated in NCM." },
            { "label": "Rampa Rebellion (1922-24): Led by Alluri Sitarama Raju in the Godavari agency of Andhra against the oppressive Madras Forest Act (1882) which restricted shifting cultivation.\nSitarama Raju was captured and executed in May 1924." },
            { "label": "**Significance**: These movements highlighted the plight of tribals and contributed to the broader anti-colonial sentiment." }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "ps",
      "label": "Peasant Movements",
      "children": [
        {
          "label": "19th Century Movements",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Indigo Revolt (1859-60): Bengal.\nLed by Digambar Biswas and Bishnucharan Biswas.\nIntellectual support from Dinabandhu Mitra's play 'Nil Darpan' (1860).\nForced the passage of the Indigo Act 1862." },
            { "label": "Pabna Agrarian Leagues (1870s): Bengal.\nLegal and peaceful rent resistance against abusive Zamindars.\nResulted in the Bengal Tenancy Act (1885)." },
            { "label": "Deccan Riots (1875): Maharashtra.\nDirected specifically against Marwari and Gujarati moneylenders (Sahukars); peasants burnt debt bonds.\nResulted in the Deccan Agriculturists Relief Act (1879)." },
            { "label": "**Analysis**: These 19th-century movements were often localized but showed growing peasant consciousness." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "20th Century Movements",
          "children": [
            { "label": "Kisan Sabha Movement (1920s): UP.\nAwadh Kisan Sabha formed by Baba Ramchandra against excessive rent and illegal cesses (bedakhli); J.L.\nNehru and others helped organise Oudh Kisan Sabhas." },
            { "label": "Eka Movement (1921-22): UP.\nLed by Madari Pasi; protested against extraction of rent above recorded rates and forced labour (begar)." },
