                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Charter Act 1813",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "EIC's trade monopoly ended EXCEPT tea & trade with China",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "₹1 Lakh annually for education (revival of literature and encouragement of sciences)",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Christian missionaries permitted to propagate religion",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Local governments empowered to impose taxes on persons and punish for non-payment",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Asserted absolute sovereignty of the British Crown over company territories in India",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Charter Act 1833",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Governor‑General of Bengal → Governor‑General of India (William Bentinck) – final step toward centralization",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Exclusive legislative powers for all British India vested in GG‑in‑Council; Madras & Bombay deprived; laws previously called Regulations, now called Acts",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "EIC became purely administrative body; commercial activities ended entirely",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Law member (Macaulay) added as 4th member without voting rights; Indian Law Commission appointed for codification",
                "kind": "fact"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Section 87 attempted open competition for civil services stating no Indian should be barred by race/religion, but negated due to fierce Court of Directors opposition",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Charter Act 1853",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Separated legislative & executive functions of GG's council → Indian Legislative Council (6 new Legislative Councillors); functioned as mini-Parliament with same procedures as British Parliament",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Introduced open competitive system for ICS (Macaulay Committee 1854) – Indians eligible; deprived Court of Directors of patronage",
                "kind": "fact"
              },
              {
                "label": "Local representation: 4 of 6 new members nominated by local govts of Madras, Bombay, Bengal, NW Provinces",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Last Charter Act; had no fixed period or timeline – indicated Company rule could be terminated by Parliament at any time",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Crown Rule (1858–1947)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Government of India Act 1858 (Act for the Good Government of India)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Abolished EIC; power directly transferred to British Crown",
                "kind": "fact"
              },
              {
                "label": "Secretary of State for India (British Cabinet member) + Council of India (15 members, advisory, chaired by SoS); SoS made a corporate body capable of suing and being sued",
