  {
    "id": "III",
    "p": "pm3",
    "label": "The Preamble – Air‑1 Complete",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Text, Source & Philosophy of the Preamble",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Exact text (opening words): “WE, THE PEOPLE OF INDIA, having solemnly resolved to constitute India into a SOVEREIGN SOCIALIST SECULAR DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC and to secure to all its citizens: JUSTICE, social, economic and political; LIBERTY of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship; EQUALITY of status and of opportunity; and to promote among them all FRATERNITY assuring the dignity of the individual and the unity and integrity of the Nation.\nIN OUR CONSTITUENT ASSEMBLY this twenty‑sixth day of November, 1949, do HEREBY ADOPT, ENACT AND GIVE TO OURSELVES THIS CONSTITUTION.”",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Source of authority: “We, the People of India” – embodies the principle of Popular Sovereignty.\nThe Constitution derives its authority from the people of India, not from any external grant or parliamentary supremacy.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Date of adoption: 26th November 1949 (Constitution Day).\nTRAP: The date of commencement is 26th January 1950, but the Preamble states the date of adoption by the Constituent Assembly.\nThe Preamble was enacted after the rest of the Constitution was already passed.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Objectives Resolution: Moved by Jawaharlal Nehru on 13 December 1946, which formed the basis of the Preamble.\nAmended by adding “and integrity” (42nd CAA) to reflect unity and integrity.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Key Words & Their Detailed Meanings",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "SOVEREIGN",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "India is internally supreme (no internal authority above the State) and externally independent (not subject to any external control).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Power to acquire foreign territory and cede a part of its territory to a foreign state (as per constitutional procedure).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Membership of the Commonwealth does not affect sovereignty; it is a voluntary association of free nations.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "SOCIALIST",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Added by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Indian socialism is democratic socialism (mixed economy, state intervention to ensure welfare and reduce inequalities) – NOT communist socialism (abolition of private property, state ownership of all means of production).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Aims to end poverty, disease, and inequality of opportunity.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Even before 1976, the Constitution contained socialist principles in the Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV), e.g., Art 39(b) and (c).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "SECULAR",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Added by the 42nd Amendment, 1976.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "India follows positive secularism: all religions enjoy equal respect and support from the State.\nThe State does not distance itself from all religions, but treats them equitably.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Not the “wall of separation” model of the US (negative secularism); the Indian State can intervene in religious affairs for social reform (Art 25(2)).",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "Secularism is a Basic Structure of the Constitution (S.R.\nBommai v.\nUnion of India, 1994).",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "DEMOCRATIC",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Based on popular sovereignty and representative indirect democracy exercised through universal adult franchise.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "India has NOT adopted direct democratic instruments like referendum, initiative, recall, or plebiscite (though some provisions of direct participation exist at local levels).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "The democratic system encompasses not just political democracy but also social and economic democracy.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "REPUBLIC",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Head of state (President) is elected, not hereditary.\nNo privileged class; all public offices are open to every citizen without discrimination.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Political sovereignty is vested in the people, not in a monarch.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "JUSTICE – Social, Economic, Political",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Social justice: abolition of discrimination, equal opportunity.\nEconomic justice: equitable distribution of wealth, adequate means of livelihood.\nPolitical justice: equal access to political process (universal adult franchise).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Inspired by the French Revolution ideal; adopted from the Russian Revolution's comprehensive concept.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "LIBERTY – of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Ensured through Fundamental Rights under Art 19 (freedom of speech etc.), Art 25‑28 (religious freedom).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "EQUALITY – of status and of opportunity",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Ensured through Art 14‑18 (equality before law, abolition of untouchability, titles), Art 16 (equal opportunity in public employment), Art 325 (no separate electorates).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "FRATERNITY – assuring dignity of the individual and the unity and integrity of the Nation",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Unity and Integrity: term “Integrity” added by 42nd CAA, 1976.\nEarlier only “unity of the Nation”.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Fraternity is promoted by single citizenship, fundamental duties (Art 51A(e)), and promoting harmony.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Amendability & Legal Status of the Preamble",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Berubari Union Case (1960)",
            "kind": "case",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Supreme Court held that the Preamble is NOT a part of the Constitution and cannot be amended under Article 368.\nIt merely serves as a key to open the minds of the framers, but it does not confer any substantive power.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Kesavananda Bharati v.\nState of Kerala (1973)",
            "kind": "case",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Overruled Berubari.\nHeld that the Preamble IS an integral part of the Constitution.\nIt can be amended under Article 368, provided the basic structure of the Constitution is not destroyed.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "The basic structure doctrine emerged from this case, and the Preamble forms a vital part of that structure.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "LIC of India v.\nConsumer Education & Research Centre (1995)",
            "kind": "case",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Reiterated that the Preamble is an integral part of the Constitution.",
                "kind": "fact"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Amendments to the Preamble",
            "kind": "amendment",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Amended ONLY ONCE: by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976, which added three words: “Socialist”, “Secular”, and “Integrity” (to “unity and integrity of the Nation”).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: No other amendment has changed the Preamble.\nThe date (26 Nov 1949) remains unchanged.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Nature of the Preamble",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Non‑justiciable: its provisions are not enforceable in a court of law.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Neither a source of power to the legislature nor a prohibition upon legislative powers.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "A key to open the minds of the framers; aids interpretation of the Constitution.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "High‑Yield TRAP Compendium for Prelims",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Date in Preamble: 26 November 1949 (adoption), NOT 26 January 1950 (commencement).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Words added by 42nd CAA: Socialist, Secular, and Integrity (not the whole phrase “unity and integrity”, only “and integrity” added).",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Is the Preamble part of the Constitution? Yes, as per Kesavananda Bharati (1973).",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Can it be amended? Yes, under Art 368, but without destroying basic structure.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Is it justiciable? No.\nBut it helps interpretation.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Does India have a referendum/recall? No direct democracy instruments at the national level.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Meaning of Secular: positive secularism; state can interfere in religion for reform.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Liberty, Equality, Fraternity: derived from French Revolution.\nJustice: social, economic, political – adopted from Russian Revolution.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Sovereign: India can cede territory to a foreign state (Berubari case).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Republic: head of state is elected; no hereditary ruler.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "IV",
    "p": "pm2",
    "label": "Union and its Territory (Art 1-4)",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Article 1 & 2: Territory and Admission",
        "kind": "article",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Article 1: India, that is Bharat, a Union of States",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Classification of territory under Article 1(3):\n\n1.\nTerritories of the States.\n2.\nUnion Territories.\n3.\nTerritories that may be acquired by the Government of India at any time.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: 'Territory of India' is a wider expression than 'Union of India'; Union includes only states sharing federal powers, Territory includes States, UTs, and future acquired territories",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "Described as 'Union' and not 'Federation':\n\n1.\nDr.\nAmbedkar emphasized that the Union is indestructible.\n2.\nStates do not have a right to secede.\n3.\nThe Indian Union is not the result of a compact or agreement between states.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Article 2: Admission of New States",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Admission/establishment of new states not previously part of India (Parliament by simple majority; concerns states external to India)",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Article 3 & 4: Reorganization and Ceding",
        "kind": "article",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Article 3: Internal Reorganization",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Formation of new states and alteration of areas, boundaries, or names of existing states (internal reorganization)",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Procedural Conditions:\n\n1.\nA bill can be introduced in either House only on the prior recommendation of the President.\n2.\nBefore recommending it, the President must refer the bill to the state legislature concerned for expressing its views within a specified period.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: President/Parliament is NOT bound by the views of the state legislature; views can be accepted or rejected even if received on time",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: No fresh reference is needed to be made to the state legislature every time an amendment to the bill is moved and accepted in Parliament",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "For Union Territories, no reference to concerned legislature is needed; Parliament can take action single-handedly",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Article 4 & Ceding Territory (TRAP Zone)",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Laws made under Articles 2 and 3 are NOT deemed amendments of the Constitution under Article 368 → can be passed by Simple Majority and normal legislative process",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Berubari Union (1960) TRAP: SC held that the power of Parliament to diminish the area of a state (under Art 3) does NOT cover ceding Indian territory to a foreign nation.\nCeding territory requires a Constitutional Amendment Act under Art 368 (e.g., 9th CAA for Berubari, 100th CAA for Bangladesh enclaves)",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Settlement of a boundary dispute between India and another country does NOT require a constitutional amendment; can be executed by executive action (SC 1969)",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "100th CAA (2015): Gave constitutional effect to the Land Boundary Agreement with Bangladesh; altered the First Schedule for Assam, West Bengal, Meghalaya, and Tripura",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "State Reorganization History",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Committees & Commissions",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "S.K.\nDhar Commission (1948): Appointed to examine linguistic reorganization; recommended reorganization on basis of administrative convenience rather than language",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "JVP Committee (1948): Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel, Pattabhi Sitaramayya; formally rejected language as the basis for reorganization of states",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Potti Sriramulu's 56-day fast led to creation of first linguistic state: Andhra State (1953) by separating Telugu-speaking areas from Madras State",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Fazl Ali Commission / States Reorganisation Commission (1953): Fazl Ali, K.M.\nPanikkar, H.N.\nKunzru.\nBroadly accepted language as a major basis but rejected 'one language-one state' theory.\nStressed preservation of unity, financial viability, and administrative efficiency",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Acts & Current Status",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "States Reorganisation Act 1956 & 7th CAA 1956: Abolished the four-fold classification (Part A, B, C, D states); created 14 states and 6 Union territories",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Parliament can reduce a state to UT status (e.g., J&K in 2019 via J&K Reorganisation Act); Constitution does not explicitly prevent downgrading a state",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "Currently 28 states & 8 UTs after J&K reorganisation (2019) creating J&K and Ladakh as separate UTs; Telangana created as 29th state in 2014 before J&K downgrade",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "V",
    "p": "pu",
    "label": "Citizenship (Art 5‑11) – Air‑1 Complete",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Constitutional Provisions (Part II, Art 5‑11)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Nature & Scope",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 5‑11 deal ONLY with citizenship status at the commencement of the Constitution (26 Jan 1950).\nThey do NOT lay down a permanent law for future acquisition or loss.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 11: Parliament is vested with plenary power to make any provision with respect to the acquisition and termination of citizenship and all other matters relating to citizenship.\nParliament enacted the Citizenship Act, 1955.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The Constitution itself does not define 'citizenship' for the post‑1950 period; that is left entirely to the Citizenship Act, 1955.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 5 – Citizenship at Commencement (Domicile Test)",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "A person became a citizen of India on 26 Jan 1950 if he had his domicile in India AND fulfilled at least one of: (a) born in India; (b) either of his parents born in India; (c) ordinarily resident in India for at least 5 years immediately before the commencement.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Domicile means permanent home + intention to reside indefinitely.\nA person can have only one domicile at a time.\nIndia has a single citizenship; there is no separate State domicile for citizenship, but domicile for certain purposes like admission to educational institutions or reservation is permissible.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 6 – Rights of Migrants from Pakistan",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "If migrated to India before 19 July 1948: automatically a citizen if he had been ordinarily resident in India since the date of migration.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "If migrated on or after 19 July 1948: must apply for registration, and before making such application, must have been resident in India for at least 6 months.\nRegistration was done by an officer appointed by the Government of India.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The cut‑off date (19 July 1948) is crucial.\nIt is not the same as the date of partition (15 Aug 1947).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 7 – Rights of Migrants to Pakistan who Later Returned",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "A person who migrated from India to Pakistan after 1 March 1947 ceased to be a citizen of India.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "If such a person returned to India under a permit for resettlement or permanent return (issued by a competent authority), he can acquire citizenship under the same conditions as Art 6 (i.e., after 6 months’ residence).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 8 – Persons of Indian Origin Residing Outside India",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Any person who, or either of whose parents or grandparents, was born in India as defined in the Government of India Act 1935, but who is ordinarily residing outside India, can claim Indian citizenship by registration with the diplomatic or consular representative of India in that country.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "This was intended to give citizenship to the Indian diaspora who wished to return.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 9 – Single Citizenship; No Dual Citizenship",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "No person shall be a citizen of India by virtue of Art 5, or deemed to be a citizen of India by virtue of Art 6 or 8, if he has voluntarily acquired the citizenship of any foreign State.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This entrenches the principle of single citizenship.\nIndia does not allow dual citizenship under the Constitution.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Overseas Citizenship of India (OCI) is NOT dual citizenship; it does not confer political rights (vote, hold constitutional office, etc.).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 10 – Continuance of Citizenship",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Every person who is a citizen of India under any of the foregoing provisions shall continue to be such citizen, subject to the provisions of any law made by Parliament.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "This means the Citizenship Act can deprive a person of citizenship under certain conditions.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Citizenship Act, 1955 – Acquisition of Citizenship",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "By Birth (Section 3)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Born on/after 26 Jan 1950 but before 1 July 1987: citizen irrespective of parents’ nationality (jus soli).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Born on/after 1 July 1987 but before 3 Dec 2004: citizen only if either parent is an Indian citizen at the time of birth.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Born on/after 3 Dec 2004: citizen if both parents are Indian citizens OR one parent is a citizen and the other is not an illegal migrant at the time of birth.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Children born to foreign diplomats or enemy aliens cannot acquire Indian citizenship by birth.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The Citizenship (Amendment) Act 2019 granted an exception to illegal migrants belonging to specified communities (Hindus, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains, Parsis, Christians) from Afghanistan, Pakistan, Bangladesh who entered India on or before 31 Dec 2014; they are not treated as illegal migrants and may apply for citizenship by naturalisation after 5 years’ residence.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Descent (Section 4)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Born outside India between 26 Jan 1950 and 10 Dec 1992: citizen if father was an Indian citizen at time of birth.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Born outside India on/after 10 Dec 1992: citizen if either parent is an Indian citizen at time of birth.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Born outside India on/after 3 Dec 2004: NOT a citizen by descent unless the birth is registered at an Indian consulate within 1 year (or with permission of the Central Government).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The requirement of registration within one year is strict; otherwise, the child does not become a citizen automatically.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Registration (Section 5)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Central Government may register a person (not an illegal migrant) as a citizen if he belongs to specified categories, e.g., PIO ordinarily resident in India for 7 years before application; person married to an Indian citizen and ordinarily resident for 7 years; minor children of Indian citizens; etc.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Must take oath of allegiance.\nMust have knowledge of one language in the Eighth Schedule.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Registration is not automatic; it’s discretionary.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Naturalisation (Section 6)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "A foreigner (not illegal migrant) may apply for naturalisation if he has: (a) resided in India for 12 months immediately preceding the application; and (b) during 14 years before that 12 months, resided in India for at least 11 years.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Must renounce previous citizenship, be of good character, and have adequate knowledge of an Eighth Schedule language.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The Central Government may waive all residence conditions if the person has rendered distinguished service to science, philosophy, art, literature, world peace, or human progress.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Under the 2019 CAA, for the specified communities from the three countries, the 11‑year requirement is reduced to 5 years (aggregate residence) for naturalisation.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Incorporation of Territory (Section 7)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "If a foreign territory becomes part of India, the Central Government may by order specify the persons who shall become citizens of India (e.g., Citizenship Order 1962 for Pondicherry, 1961 for Goa, Daman and Diu, etc.).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Loss of Citizenship (Citizenship Act, 1955)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "By Renunciation (Section 8)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "An adult citizen may voluntarily renounce Indian citizenship.\nIf a parent renounces, all minor children cease to be citizens; but such child may resume Indian citizenship within one year of attaining majority.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Government can suspend the right to renounce during a war in which India is engaged.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Termination (Section 9)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Automatically ceases when an Indian citizen voluntarily acquires the citizenship of another country.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This provision does NOT apply during a war in which India is engaged (to prevent mass loss of loyalty).",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "If a question arises whether a person has acquired foreign citizenship, it is determined by the Central Government and its decision is final (subject to judicial review).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "By Deprivation (Section 10)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Central Government may compulsorily terminate citizenship obtained by registration or naturalisation on grounds of: (a) fraud/misrepresentation; (b) disloyalty/disaffection to the Constitution; (c) unlawful trade or communication with an enemy during war; (d) imprisonment in any country for ≥2 years within 5 years of registration/naturalisation; (e) ordinary residence outside India continuously for 7 years.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Deprivation does not apply to a person who is a citizen by birth or descent, only to those who acquired citizenship by registration/naturalisation.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Special Provisions – Assam Accord & Section 6A (Citizenship Act)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Inserted in 1985 to give effect to the Assam Accord (1985).\nDeals with persons of Indian origin who entered Assam from Bangladesh (then East Pakistan) before 25 March 1971.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Those entering before 1 Jan 1966: deemed citizens of India from the date of entry.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Those entering between 1 Jan 1966 and 25 March 1971: detected as foreigners and registered; citizenship after 10 years from detection, with no voting rights for those 10 years.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: Supreme Court upheld the constitutional validity of Section 6A in 2024 (by a 4:1 majority), holding that the cut-off date of 25 March 1971 was reasonable and balanced the humanitarian approach with the protection of the Assamese people.",
            "kind": "trap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Overseas Citizenship of India (OCI) – Card Scheme",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "OCI is NOT dual citizenship.\nIt is a long‑term visa/status for Persons of Indian Origin (PIOs) who have taken foreign citizenship.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Introduced by Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2005.\nPIO scheme merged with OCI in 2015.",
            "kind": "fact"
          },
          {
            "label": "OCI cardholders:\n\nEnjoy:\n1.\nMultiple-entry lifelong visa.\n2.\nExemption from registration with FRRO.\n3.\nParity with NRIs in economic, financial, and educational fields, except for agricultural and plantation land.\n\nDo NOT have:\n1.\nRight to vote.\n2.\nRight to hold constitutional offices such as President, Vice-President, or Judge of the SC/HC.\n3.\nRight to be a member of Parliament or a State Legislature.\n4.\nRight to government employment.",
            "kind": "numbered"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: OCI card can be cancelled if the person violates Indian laws or shows disaffection to the Constitution.",
            "kind": "trap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Key Amendments to the Citizenship Act",
        "kind": "amendment",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "1986: Tightened birthright citizenship – added condition that one parent must be Indian citizen for those born after 1 July 1987.",
            "kind": "fact"
          },
          {
            "label": "1992: Extended descent through mother as well (earlier only father).",
            "kind": "fact"
          },
          {
            "label": "2003: Further tightened birthright citizenship – both parents must be citizens or one parent citizen and other not illegal migrant.\nIntroduced OCI registration instead of earlier PIO card.\nMandated National Register of Citizens (NRC).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "2005: Introduced OCI as a scheme; separate PIO scheme continued.",
            "kind": "fact"
          },
          {
            "label": "2015: Merged PIO with OCI; amended definition of OCI.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "2019: Citizenship (Amendment) Act (CAA) – granted eligibility for citizenship by naturalisation to illegal migrants who are Hindus, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains, Parsis or Christians from Afghanistan, Bangladesh, Pakistan, who entered India on or before 31 Dec 2014.\nReduced residence requirement from 11 years to 5 years.\nExempted tribal areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Tripura (Sixth Schedule areas) and Inner Line Permit areas from its operation.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Important Judicial Interpretations",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Berubari Union Case (1960): Cession of Indian territory to another country does not automatically deprive citizens of that area of their Indian citizenship.\nParliament must pass a law.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Pradeep Jain v.\nUnion of India (1984): ‘Domicile’ for purposes of state public employment is not equivalent to citizenship; residence requirements must be reasonable.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "National Register of Citizens (Assam): Upheld by SC; process to identify illegal migrants based on 1971 cut‑off.",
            "kind": "fact"
          },
          {
            "label": "CAA 2019 validity pending before SC; however, the Supreme Court has not stayed its operation.\nThe 2024 judgment on Section 6A reaffirmed that Parliament can create different classes of migrants.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "High‑Yield TRAP Compendium",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Single citizenship: Art 9.\nOCI is not dual citizenship.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 5: domicile + (a)/(b)/(c).\nArt 11: Parliament's law supreme for post‑1950 citizenship.",
            "kind": "article"
          },
          {
            "label": "Migration cut‑off under Art 6: 19 July 1948.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Renunciation: war exception.\nTermination: automatic foreign citizenship, not applicable during war.",
            "kind": "exception"
          },
          {
            "label": "Deprivation: only for registration/naturalisation, not birth/descent.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Birthright citizenship phases: 1 July 1987, 3 Dec 2004.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Descent: registration within 1 year for those born abroad after 3 Dec 2004.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Assam Accord: Section 6A, cut‑off 25 March 1971.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "CAA 2019: eligibility for 6 communities from 3 countries, residence reduced to 5 years; exempts Sixth Schedule & ILP areas.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "OCI: no political rights, no agricultural/plantation land, cannot hold constitutional posts.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Domicile vs citizenship: State domicile is not a constitutional concept, but residence‑based benefits may be allowed.",
            "kind": "comparison"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "VI",
    "p": "pm3",
    "label": "Fundamental Rights (Art 12-35) – Air-1 Complete",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Art 12 & 13: State and Judicial Review",
        "kind": "article",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 12: Definition of State",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Includes:\n\n1.\nUnion & State Government Bodies - Executive and legislative organs.\n2.\nLocal Authorities - Municipalities, panchayats, district boards, improvement trusts, etc.\n3.\nStatutory & Non-Statutory Instrumentalities - LIC, ONGC, SAIL, SBI, etc.\n4.\nGovernment-Controlled Organizations - Any body financially, functionally, and administratively dominated by or under government control (Ajay Hasia test).",
                "kind": "numbered"
              },
              {
                "label": "Ajay Hasia v.\nKhalid Mujib (1981): 6-factor test for 'other authorities' under Art 12:\n\n1.\nEntire share capital is held by the government.\n2.\nSubstantial financial assistance comes from the State.\n3.\nThe body enjoys State-conferred or State-protected monopoly status.\n4.\nThe government exercises deep and pervasive control.\n5.\nThe functions are of public importance and closely related to governmental functions.\n6.\nA government department has been transferred to the corporation.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Pradeep Kumar Biswas v.\nIndian Inst.\nof Chemical Biology (2002): Overruled Sabhajit Tewary; held CSIR is 'State' under Art 12.\nClarified that the test is 'functionally, financially, and administratively dominated/controlled by government'.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Zee Telefilms v.\nUnion of India (2005): BCCI is NOT 'State' under Art 12, but as it performs public functions, it is amenable to writ jurisdiction under Art 226 (HC),NOT under Art 32.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Judiciary is 'State' when exercising administrative/rule-making functions (e.g., appointments, transfers), NOT when acting judicially (Naresh Mirajkar v.\nState of Maharashtra).",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: A private body that is neither an instrumentality nor performing public duties is not 'State'.\nNo FR enforcement against it under Art 12.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 13: Judicial Review & Doctrines",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 13(1): Pre-constitutional laws inconsistent with FRs become void to the extent of inconsistency – Doctrine of Eclipse (law dormant for citizens; can revive if FR amended to remove inconsistency).\nNon-citizens can still be governed by eclipsed law.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 13(2): Post-constitutional laws violating FRs are void ab initio (void from inception).\nCannot be revived by subsequent amendment; must be re-enacted.\nEclipse does NOT apply to post-constitutional laws (Deep Chand v.\nState of UP, 1959).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Doctrine of Severability: Only offending part struck down, valid part remains if it can stand independently (R.M.D.C.\nv.\nUnion of India, 1957).\nIf inextricably linked, entire law falls.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Doctrine of Waiver: FRs CANNOT be waived by an individual (Basheshar Nath v.\nCIT, 1959).\nEven consent cannot deprive a person of FRs.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Meaning of 'Law' under Art 13: Includes ordinances, orders, bye-laws, rules, regulations, notifications, customs having force of law.\nDoes NOT include Constitutional Amendments under Art 368 (after 24th CAA, 1971), BUT per Kesavananda (1973), even CAAs can be tested against Basic Structure (judicial review survives).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 13(3) defines 'law' to include 'custom or usage having in the territory of India the force of law'.\nNot all customs, only those recognised as law.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Equality Rights (Art 14-18)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 14: Equality before law & Equal protection of laws",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Equality Before Law\nIt originated in the UK.\n\nIt is a negative concept.\n\nIt means no one has special privileges.\n\nIt subjects everyone equally to the ordinary law (Rule of Law).\n\nEqual Protection of Laws\nIt originated in the US.\n\nIt is a positive concept.\n\nIt guarantees equal treatment in equal circumstances.\n\nIt means that like should be treated alike.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Permits reasonable classification:\n\n1.\nIntelligible differentia - The grouped persons or things must be distinguishable from others left out.\n2.\nRational nexus - That differentia must have a reasonable relation to the object sought to be achieved.\n\n(State of W.B.\nv.\nAnwar Ali Sarkar, 1952)",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "New dimension: Manifest Arbitrariness Test – Any state action that is arbitrary is violative of Art 14 (E.P.\nRoyappa v.\nState of T.N., 1974; reaffirmed in Maneka Gandhi, Shayara Bano).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP Exceptions: Art 361 (President/Governors immune from criminal proceedings and arrest during term; no civil suits for acts done in official capacity without 2 months' notice), Art 361A (immunity for true publication of parliamentary/legislative proceedings), Art 31C (laws under 39(b)/(c) immune from Art 14 challenge), foreign sovereigns, diplomats, UN officials.",
                "kind": "exception"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 14 applies to ALL persons (citizens + non-citizens + legal persons like companies).\nAvailable against State action only.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 15: Prohibition of Discrimination",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 15(1): State cannot discriminate against any citizen on grounds ONLY of religion, race, caste, sex, place of birth, or any of them.\nTRAP: 'Only' means other grounds like residence CAN be used (e.g., domicile-based quotas for State education not per se void).",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 15(2): Horizontal application – no citizen shall be subject to disability regarding access to shops, public restaurants, hotels, places of public entertainment, use of wells, tanks, roads, places of public resort maintained wholly or partly out of state funds or dedicated to public use.\nApplies against BOTH State and private individuals.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Exceptions:\n\n1.Art 15(3) - Special provisions for women and children.\n2.Art 15(4) - Advancement of SEBCs and SCs/STs, inserted by the 1st CAA.\n3.Art 15(5) - Reservation in admissions to educational institutions, including private unaided institutions, for SEBCs, SCs, and STs, except minority institutions.\n4.Art 15(6) - 10% EWS reservation in educational institutions, inserted by the 103rd CAA, 2019.",
                "kind": "exception"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 15(5) upheld in Ashoka Kumar Thakur v.\nUnion of India (2008) but excluded 'creamy layer' from OBC reservation in higher education.\nEWS reservation upheld by 3:2 majority in Janhit Abhiyan v.\nUnion of India (2022).",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Minority institutions (Art 30) are completely exempt from Art 15(5) reservation (Society for Unaided Private Schools of Rajasthan v.\nUOI, 2012).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 16: Equality of Opportunity in Public Employment",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Prohibited grounds: Religion, race, caste, sex, descent, place of birth, residence, or any of them.\nTRAP: 'Descent' includes hereditary office; 'residence' can be prescribed by Parliament under Art 16(3).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 16(3): ONLY Parliament (not state legislature) may prescribe residence within a State/UT as a condition for certain classes of employment.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Exceptions:\n16(4) reservation for 'backward class of citizens' not adequately represented (includes SC/ST/OBC – Indra Sawhney).\n16(4A) reservation in promotion with consequential seniority for SC/ST (77th CAA, 1995; 85th CAA, 2001).\n16(4B) carry-forward of unfilled reserved vacancies beyond 50% ceiling in a year (81st CAA, 2000).\n16(5) office in religious/denominational institution can be reserved for adherents.\n16(6) 10% EWS reservation (103rd CAA, 2019).",
                "kind": "exception"
              },
              {
                "label": "Indra Sawhney v.\nUOI (Mandal Case, 1992):\n\n1.\nReservation is valid in public employment.\n2.\n50% ceiling generally applies, except in extraordinary situations.\n3.\nCreamy layer exclusion applies to OBCs.\n4.\nNo reservation in promotions under the original ruling (later altered by the 77th CAA).\n5.\nReservation is not a Fundamental Right.\n6.\n'Backward class' includes SC/ST/OBC categories in the broader reservation discourse.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "M.Nagaraj v.\nUOI (2006): Upheld 77th/81st/85th CAAs with conditions – State must show (i) backwardness, (ii) inadequacy of representation, (iii) maintenance of efficiency; creamy layer applies to SC/ST in promotions (Jarnail Singh v.\nLachhmi Narain Gupta, 2018 extended it).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 16(6) EWS reservation cannot be on grounds covered by Art 16(4) (i.e., EWS only for those not already covered by SC/ST/OBC reservation).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 17: Abolition of Untouchability",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Absolute right, no exceptions; applies against State and private individuals.\nEnforced by Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 (punishes practice of untouchability).\nTRAP: 'Untouchability' is NOT defined in Constitution or Act.",
                "kind": "exception"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Discrimination on basis of untouchability also violates Art 14, 15, 21.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 18: Abolition of Titles",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Article 18 restrictions:\n\n1.\nThe State cannot confer titles, except military and academic distinctions.\n2.\nCitizens cannot accept titles from foreign states.\n3.\nA foreigner holding any office of profit or trust under the State needs the President's consent to accept a foreign title.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              },
              {
                "label": "National Awards (Bharat Ratna, Padma) are decorations, not titles (Balaji Raghavan v.\nUOI, 1996).\nBut awardees cannot use them as prefixes/suffixes (violates Art 14/18).\nTRAP: The awards themselves are not unconstitutional; only their use as titles is restricted.",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Right to Freedom (Art 19-22)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 19: Six Democratic Freedoms (originally 7; right to property 19(1)(f) removed by 44th CAA 1978)",
            "kind": "amendment",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "All rights under Art 19 available ONLY to citizens.\nProtected against State action only.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(a) Speech & Expression – Implied rights: freedom of press, right to information, right to know about government decisions, right to commercial speech (Tata Press), right to broadcast, right to criticise, right to remain silent (Bijoe Emmanuel).\nRestrictions under 19(2): sovereignty & integrity of India, security of state, friendly relations with foreign states, public order, decency/morality, contempt of court, defamation, incitement to an offence.\nTRAP: 'Public order' added by 1st CAA (Romesh Thappar, 1950).\nSection 66A IT Act struck down in Shreya Singhal v.\nUOI (2015) for being overbroad and violating 19(1)(a).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(b) Assemble peaceably without arms – Restrictions (19(3)): sovereignty/integrity, public order.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(c) Form associations/unions/cooperative societies (97th CAA, 2011 added cooperatives) – Restrictions (19(4)): sovereignty/integrity, public order, morality.\nTRAP: Right to form association does NOT include right to achieve objectives by illegal means; no fundamental right to strike (T.K.\nRangarajan v.\nGovt.\nof TN, 2003).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(d) Move freely throughout India – Restrictions (19(5)): general public, protection of ST interests.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(e) Reside and settle in any part of India – Restrictions (19(5)): same as above.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "19(1)(g) Practice any profession, carry on occupation, trade, business – Restrictions (19(6)): general public interest, professional/technical qualifications, state monopoly (can create absolute monopoly, even to the exclusion of citizens).\nTRAP: State can nationalise trade.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Suspension: Art 358 auto-suspends Art 19 only when Emergency declared on ground of war or external aggression (NOT armed rebellion – 44th CAA).\nSuspension limited to period of Emergency and only in territory where Emergency is in force.\nDuring such suspension, no remedy under Art 19, but if Emergency proclamation is mala fide, courts may intervene.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 20: Protection in Respect of Conviction for Offences",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "20(1) Ex-post-facto law: No person (citizen/foreigner/corp) convicted of any offence except for violation of law in force at the time of act.\nPenalty cannot exceed that prescribed at the time.\nTRAP: Applies only to criminal laws; civil/tax liability may be retrospective.\nProhibition is only on conviction/sentence, not on trial or investigation.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "20(2) Double Jeopardy: No person prosecuted and punished twice for the same offence.\nTRAP: Available only before a court of law or judicial tribunal; NOT in departmental proceedings (Maqbool Hussain v.\nState of Bombay).\nAlso, if first trial was void/without jurisdiction, retrial possible.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "20(3) Self-Incrimination:\n\n1.\nNo person accused of an offence can be compelled to be a witness against himself.\n2.\nIt is not available to non-accused persons such as ordinary witnesses.\n3.\nIt does not bar compulsory thumb impressions, handwriting samples, blood samples, exhibition of the body, or voice samples.\n4.\nIt applies to oral and documentary evidence.\n5.\nIt applies only to criminal proceedings, not to civil proceedings or inquiries.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 21: Right to Life and Personal Liberty",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "A.K.\nGopalan (1950): 'Procedure established by law' meant any procedure laid down by legislature; Art 21, 19, 14 mutually exclusive.\nOverruled.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "R.C.\nCooper (Bank Nationalisation, 1970): Held Art 19 and 31 not mutually exclusive; set stage for Maneka.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Maneka Gandhi v.\nUOI (1978): 'Procedure established by law' must be just, fair, reasonable, and non-arbitrary.\nArt 14, 19, 21 form 'Golden Triangle' – any law depriving life/liberty must satisfy all three.\nRight to travel abroad is part of Art 21.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Expanded rights under Art 21: Right to livelihood (Olga Tellis), right to clean environment (M.C.\nMehta, Vellore Citizens), right to health & emergency medical aid (Parmanand Katara, Paschim Banga), right to education (Unni Krishnan before 21A), right to privacy (Puttaswamy, 2017 – 9-judge bench, inherent), right to sleep (Ram Lila Maidan), right to reputation (Kishore Samrite, BCCI), right to information (part of 19(1)(a) and 21), right to free legal aid (Hussainara Khatoon, M.H.\nHoskot), right to speedy trial (Hussainara Khatoon), right against handcuffing, solitary confinement, custodial torture (D.K.\nBasu guidelines), right to electricity (Consumer Education & Research Centre), right to internet access as part of 19(1)(a) and 21 (Anuradha Bhasin, 2020), right to marry person of one's choice (Shakti Vahini), right to passive euthanasia under strict guidelines (Common Cause v.\nUOI, 2018), right to die (active suicide) NOT included (Gian Kaur v.\nState of Punjab, 1996).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Right to privacy: Recognised as fundamental right under Art 21 in K.S.\nPuttaswamy v.\nUOI (2017).\nOverruled M.P.\nSharma and Kharak Singh.\nIncludes informational privacy, bodily integrity, decisional autonomy.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 21 is available to ALL persons (including foreigners).\nCannot be suspended even during National Emergency (44th CAA).\nADM Jabalpur (1976) held Art 21 could be suspended; this was overruled/virtually nullified by 44th CAA and later judgments.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 21A: Right to Education",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Inserted by 86th CAA, 2002.\nState shall provide free and compulsory education to all children aged 6-14 years as may be determined by law.\nRTE Act 2009 implements this.\nTRAP: Only elementary education (up to class 8).\n86th CAA also amended Art 45 (early childhood care) and Art 51A(k) (duty of parents).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "RTE Act provisions:\n\n1.\n25% reservation for disadvantaged groups in private unaided schools.\n2.\nNo-detention policy, though later amended.\n3.\nMinority institutions are exempt from this reservation.\n\n(Society for Unaided Private Schools of Rajasthan v.\nUOI, 2012)",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 22: Protection Against Arrest and Detention",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Punitive Detention safeguards (22(1)-(2)): Right to be informed of grounds of arrest (as soon as possible), right to consult and be defended by legal practitioner of choice, production before nearest magistrate within 24 hours (excluding journey time).\nTRAP: These safeguards NOT available to enemy alien or person detained under preventive detention law.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Preventive Detention: 22(4)-(7).\nMax period without Advisory Board approval: 3 months.\nTRAP: 44th CAA reduced this to 2 months BUT that provision was never notified – it remains 3 months.\nAdvisory Board must consist of judges of a HC or persons qualified to be HC judges.\nDetenu has right to be informed of grounds of detention and to make representation against detention.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Legislative power: Parliament has exclusive power to make preventive detention laws for reasons related to Defence, Foreign Affairs, or Security of India.\nBoth Parliament and State Legislatures have concurrent power for Public Order and maintenance of essential supplies/services.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Landmark cases:\n\n1.\nA.K.\nRoy v.\nUOI (1982) - Upheld the NSA but laid down procedural safeguards.\n2.\nD.K.\nBasu v.\nState of W.B.\n(1997) - Issued guidelines for arrest and detention.\n3.\nJoginder Kumar v.\nState of U.P.\n(1994) - Held that arrest must be based on reasonable justification, not routine action.",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Right Against Exploitation (Art 23-24)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 23: Prohibition of Human Trafficking & Forced Labour",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Prohibition of traffic in human beings, begar, and bonded labour.\nAvailable against State and private individuals.\nTRAP: 'Begar' means forced labour without payment.\nBonded Labour System (Abolition) Act 1976 implements Art 23.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Exception: State may impose compulsory service for public purposes (e.g., military conscription, social service during emergencies) without remuneration, but no discrimination on grounds ONLY of religion, race, caste, class.",
                "kind": "exception"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 24: Prohibition of Child Labour",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "No child below 14 years shall be employed in any factory, mine, or hazardous employment.\nTRAP: The Child Labour (Prohibition & Regulation) Amendment Act 2016 extended prohibition to all occupations for under 14; regulates 14-18 in hazardous processes.\nArt 24 is absolute, read with DPSP Art 39(e)(f).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Freedom of Religion (Art 25-28)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 25: Individual Religious Freedom",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Covers: Freedom of conscience, right to freely profess, practice, and propagate religion.\nAvailable to citizens and foreigners.\nSubject to: public order, morality, health, AND other provisions of Part III.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "State can regulate/restrict secular activities associated with religious practice (Art 25(2)(a)).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Explanation I: Wearing and carrying of kirpans deemed included in profession of Sikh religion.\nExplanation II: 'Hindus' includes Sikhs, Jains, Buddhists for purposes of Art 25.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Right to propagate does NOT include forced conversion (Stainislaus v.\nState of M.P., 1977).\nConversion by fraud, force, allurement is prohibited.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "Essential Religious Practices Doctrine: Supreme Court determines whether a practice is 'essential' to a religion to decide protection under Art 25.\nCases: Shirur Mutt (1954) – test laid down; Durgah Committee (1961) – only essential practices protected; Sabarimala (2018) – exclusion of women of menstruating age NOT essential practice, violates Art 14, 15, 25; Shayara Bano (2017) – triple talaq not essential practice of Islam, manifestly arbitrary, violates Art 14.",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 26: Denominational Rights (Corporate Religious Freedom)",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Rights under Art 26:\n\n1.\nEstablish and maintain institutions for religious and charitable purposes.\n2.\nManage its own affairs in matters of religion.\n3.\nOwn and acquire movable and immovable property.\n4.\nAdminister such property in accordance with law.\n\nRestrictions:\nOnly public order, morality, and health.\n\nTRAP:\nArt 26 is not expressly subject to the other provisions of Part III, unlike Art 25.",
                "kind": "numbered"
              },
              {
                "label": "Test for denomination: Common faith, common organisation, distinctive name (S.P.\nMittal v.\nUOI).",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 27: Freedom from Taxation for Promotion of Religion",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "State cannot compel any person to pay taxes specifically for promotion or maintenance of any particular religion/denomination.\nTRAP: Prohibits TAX, but not FEE (fees for secular regulation/safety of pilgrims permissible).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 28: Religious Instruction in Educational Institutions",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Wholly maintained by State: Religious instruction completely prohibited.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Administered by State but established under trust/endowment requiring religious instruction: Permitted.",
                "kind": "fact"
              },
              {
                "label": "Recognised by State or receiving aid: Instruction permitted but only on voluntary basis (consent of individual/guardian).",
                "kind": "fact"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Cultural, Educational & Saving Rights (Art 29-31C)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 29: Protection of Interests of Minorities",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "29(1): Any section of citizens residing in India having a distinct language, script, or culture of its own has the right to conserve the same.\nTRAP: 'Section of citizens' includes both majority and minority (D.A.V.\nCollege v.\nState of Punjab).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "29(2): No citizen denied admission into state-maintained or state-aided educational institution on grounds ONLY of religion, race, caste, language.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 30: Right of Minorities to Establish Institutions",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Available ONLY to religious and linguistic minorities.\n'Minority' not defined in Constitution; determined on basis of state population (T.M.A.\nPai Foundation, 2002).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.\nRight includes autonomy in administration, but state can regulate for educational standards and to prevent maladministration, provided minority character not destroyed.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "30(1A): Compensation for compulsory acquisition of minority educational property must not restrict the guaranteed right.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Minority institutions are exempt from Art 15(5) reservation (93rd CAA) and RTE Act's 25% reservation (2012 judgment).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Savings of Certain Laws (Art 31A, 31B, 31C)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 31A: Saves laws providing for: (a) acquisition of estates, (b) taking over management of properties, (c) amalgamation of corporations, (d) extinguishment/modification of rights of directors/shareholders, (e) extraction of minerals – from challenge under Art 14 & 19.\nInserted by 1st CAA 1951 to protect zamindari abolition laws.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 31B: Validates acts and regulations placed in the Ninth Schedule to immunize them from FR challenge (1st CAA).\nI.R.\nCoelho v.\nState of Tamil Nadu (2007): Laws added to 9th Schedule after 24 April 1973 (Kesavananda date) are open to judicial review if they violate Basic Structure (including Art 14, 19, 21).\nNo blanket immunity.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 31C: Inserted by 25th CAA 1971.\nAny law giving effect to Directive Principles under Art 39(b) or (c) (material resources distribution, prevention of wealth concentration) shall not be void for inconsistency with Art 14 or 19.\nTRAP: 42nd CAA extended this immunity to ALL DPSPs; SC struck down extension in Minerva Mills (1980), restoring protection only for 39(b)/(c).",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Constitutional Remedies & Exceptions (Art 32-35)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 32: Right to Constitutional Remedies",
            "kind": "article",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 32 is itself a Fundamental Right (Dr.\nAmbedkar: 'Heart and soul of the Constitution').\nCan be invoked ONLY for enforcement of Fundamental Rights (not ordinary legal rights).\nSC's jurisdiction original but not exclusive (concurrent with HC under Art 226).",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Locus standi liberalised under PIL: Public Interest Litigation allows any public-spirited person to approach SC for enforcement of FRs of others who cannot due to disability/poverty (S.P.\nGupta, 1981).\nTRAP: Strict locus standi does not apply to Habeas Corpus and Quo Warranto.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Res Judicata applies to writ petitions except Habeas Corpus (which can be filed multiple times if detention continues unlawfully).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Suspension: Art 32 remedy can be suspended by Presidential order under Art 359 during National Emergency, except for enforcement of Art 20 & 21 (44th CAA).\nArt 358 auto-suspends Art 19 remedy during war/external aggression emergency.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Writs (Art 32 & 226)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Habeas Corpus ('to have the body'): Secure release of person illegally detained.\nLies against State and private individuals.\nCan be filed by detainee or anyone on his behalf.\nTRAP: Not issued if detention is lawful, under valid legislative authority, or outside court's jurisdiction.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Mandamus ('we command'): Command to public official/body/court to perform a mandatory public duty.\nTRAP: Not issued against President/Governor for exercise of constitutional discretion, private individuals, CJI acting judicially, Parliament/State Legislature (internal proceedings), or purely discretionary functions.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Prohibition ('to forbid'): Preventive; superior court forbids inferior court/tribunal from exceeding jurisdiction or acting contrary to law.\nLies only against judicial/quasi-judicial bodies.\nIssued before order.\nTRAP: Not against administrative bodies.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Certiorari ('to be certified'): Curative; quashes illegal order already passed by inferior court/tribunal.\nAvailable against judicial/quasi-judicial AND administrative bodies (since 1991) if they affect rights.\nTRAP: Certiorari can be issued after order; Prohibition before.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Quo Warranto ('by what authority'): Enquires legality of claim to a substantive public office of permanent character (created by statute/Constitution).\nAny person can file (locus standi relaxed).\nTRAP: Not available for purely private office, ministerial office, or office removable at pleasure.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Exceptions to FRs (Art 33-35)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 33: Only Parliament can restrict/abrogate FRs of members of Armed Forces, Police, or intelligence organisations to ensure proper discharge of duties and discipline.\nLaws made under Art 33 cannot be challenged as violating FRs.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 34: Restriction of FRs while Martial Law is in force in any area.\nParliament may indemnify acts done under Martial Law.\nTRAP: Martial Law is NOT defined in Constitution; distinct from National Emergency (Art 352).",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 35: Only Parliament (not state legislatures) can make penal laws for offences under Art 17 (untouchability), Art 23 (trafficking), Art 24 (child labour), and Art 16(3) (residence condition for employment).\nAlso, Parliament has exclusive power to make laws prescribing maximum period of preventive detention (beyond 3 months) for certain purposes.",
                "kind": "article"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Rights Available to Citizens vs.\nNon-Citizens",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Available to ALL persons (Citizens + Foreigners): Art 14, 20, 21, 21A, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28.\nTRAP: Enemy aliens excluded from Art 22 protections.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Available ONLY to Citizens: Art 15, 16, 19, 29, 30.\n(Also Art 326 – right to vote, though it is a constitutional right, not FR; right to contest elections is a statutory right under Representation of People Act).",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Key Amendments Related to Fundamental Rights (Chronological)",
            "kind": "amendment",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "1st CAA, 1951: Added Art 15(4) (SC/ST reservation in education), Art 31A, Art 31B (9th Schedule), amended Art 19(2) to add 'public order', etc.\nResponded to Champakam Dorairajan & Kameshwar Singh.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "24th CAA, 1971: Amended Art 13 to exclude CAAs from definition of 'law'; affirmed Parliament's power to amend FRs (overruled Golaknath in part).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "25th CAA, 1971: Inserted Art 31C protecting laws under Art 39(b)/(c); also changed 'compensation' to 'amount' in Art 31 (now repealed).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "42nd CAA, 1976: Extended Art 31C immunity to all DPSPs (struck down in Minerva Mills).\nAlso attempted to curtail Art 32 writ jurisdiction (restored by 43rd/44th).",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "44th CAA, 1978:\n\n1.\nRemoved the Right to Property from Fundamental Rights by deleting Art 19(1)(f) and Art 31; it now survives under Art 300A.\n2.\nAmended Art 22 on preventive detention, including the unnotified 2-month change.\n3.\nMade Art 20 and Art 21 non-suspendable during Emergency.\n4.\nRestored suspension of Art 19 only in case of war or external aggression, not armed rebellion.\n5.\nEnsured that the Art 32 remedy for Art 20 and Art 21 cannot be suspended.",
                "kind": "case"
              },
              {
                "label": "77th CAA, 1995: Art 16(4A) – reservation in promotion for SC/ST.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "81st CAA, 2000: Art 16(4B) – carry-forward of unfilled reserved vacancies.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "85th CAA, 2001: Consequential seniority for SC/ST in promotion.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "86th CAA, 2002: Art 21A (RTE), amended Art 45, Art 51A(k).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "93rd CAA, 2005: Art 15(5) – reservation in private unaided educational institutions (except minority).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "97th CAA, 2011: Amended Art 19(1)(c) – added co-operative societies; added Part IXB.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "103rd CAA, 2019: Art 15(6) & 16(6) – 10% EWS reservation.\nUpheld in Janhit Abhiyan (2022).",
                "kind": "case"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "VII",
    "p": "pm3",
    "label": "Directive Principles of State Policy (Art 36‑51) – Air‑1 Complete",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Nature & General Provisions (Art 36‑37)",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 36: Definition of State – same as Art 12; includes Union & State Govts, Parliament & Legislatures, local authorities, and instrumentalities.\nTRAP: DPSPs impose obligations on the 'State' as defined in Art 12, so they apply to all public authorities.",
            "kind": "article"
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 37: DPSPs are non‑justiciable (cannot be enforced by any court), but they are 'fundamental in the governance of the country'.\nIt is the DUTY of the State to apply these principles in making laws.",
            "kind": "article"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: Non‑justiciability does NOT mean DPSPs are toothless.\nCourts use them to: (1) interpret Fundamental Rights, (2) test reasonableness of restrictions under Art 19, (3) resolve ambiguities in statutes, (4) uphold laws that implement DPSPs as being in public interest.\n(Unni Krishnan, AIIMS Students Union case)",
            "kind": "trap"
          },
          {
            "label": "Borrowed from: Irish Constitution (which took them from the Spanish Constitution).\nPrecursor: 'Instruments of Instructions' under the GoI Act 1935.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: The classification into Socialist, Gandhian, and Liberal‑Intellectual is ACADEMIC – the Constitution does NOT group DPSPs in this way.\nStill, it is heavily tested in Prelims.",
            "kind": "trap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Academic Classification of DPSPs",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Socialist Principles: Art 38, 39, 39A, 41, 42, 43, 43A, 47 – aim to establish a welfare state and reduce inequality.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Gandhian Principles: Art 40, 43 (cottage industries part), 46, 47 (prohibition of intoxicants), 48 (organisation of agriculture & cow slaughter prohibition) – reflect Gandhian ideals of self‑sufficiency and social reform.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Liberal‑Intellectual Principles: Art 44 (UCC), 45, 48A, 49, 50, 51 – represent the liberal, reformist, and internationalist outlook.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Key Constitutional Amendments Related to DPSPs",
        "kind": "amendment",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "25th CAA 1971: Inserted Art 31C – laws giving effect to Art 39(b)&(c) immune from challenge under Art 14 & 19 (this is Part III, but directly ties DPSPs to FR limitation).\nTRAP: 42nd CAA extended it to ALL DPSPs – struck down in Minerva Mills.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "42nd CAA 1976: Added Art 39(f) [healthy development of children], Art 39A [equal justice & free legal aid], Art 43A [workers' participation in management], Art 48A [environment & wildlife protection].\nTRAP: These are often asked as 'which DPSPs were added by the 42nd Amendment?'",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "44th CAA 1978: Added Art 38(2) – State to minimize inequalities in income, status, facilities and opportunities.\nAlso restored some FR positions, but for DPSPs this is the key addition.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "86th CAA 2002: Amended Art 45 – changed its subject from free & compulsory education for children up to 14 years (now an FR under Art 21A) to early childhood care & education for children under 6 years.\nTRAP: Know the exact shift; earlier Art 45 covered 6‑14, now Art 21A covers 6‑14 and Art 45 covers 0‑6.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "97th CAA 2011: Added Art 43B – promotion of voluntary formation, autonomous functioning, and democratic control of co‑operative societies.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Article‑wise DPSPs with Key Points, Cases & Traps",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Socialist Directives (Art 38 – 43B)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 38(1): State shall strive to promote welfare by securing a social order based on justice – social, economic and political.\nArt 38(2) [added by 44th CAA]: minimise inequalities in income, status, facilities, opportunities among individuals and groups.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 39: State shall direct its policy towards securing – (a) adequate means of livelihood for all citizens; (b) distribution of ownership/control of material resources to subserve the common good; (c) prevention of concentration of wealth; (d) equal pay for equal work for both men and women; (e) health and strength of workers, and protection of children from abuse; (f) children given opportunities and facilities to develop healthily, and protection of youth against exploitation and moral/ material abandonment [added by 42nd CAA].",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Art 39(b)&(c) are the ONLY DPSPs that, via Art 31C, enjoy primacy over Fundamental Rights under Art 14 & 19.\nThis is a favourite UPSC elimination point.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 39A: Equal justice and free legal aid [42nd CAA].\nImplemented through the Legal Services Authorities Act 1987 (NALSA).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 41: Right to work, to education, and to public assistance in cases of unemployment, old age, sickness and disablement.\nBasis for MNREGA (right to work), National Social Assistance Programme.\nTRAP: It is a directive, not a justiciable right.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 42: Just and humane conditions of work and maternity relief.\nThe Maternity Benefit Act 1961 (amended 2017) draws inspiration from this.\nTRAP: It applies to all workers, including informal sector, but the directive itself is aspirational.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 43: Living wage, decent standard of life, full enjoyment of leisure and social and cultural opportunities; promotion of cottage industries (Gandhian element).\nTRAP: The 'living wage' is a step above 'minimum wage', which is a statutory concept.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 43A: Workers' participation in management [42nd CAA].\nImplemented through inclusion of worker directors in certain PSUs and schemes like Works Committees.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 43B: Promotion of co‑operative societies [97th CAA].\nMandates State to promote voluntary formation, autonomous functioning, democratic control and professional management of co‑operatives.\nTRAP: This is a directive, but Part IXB (Articles 243ZH‑243ZT) gives constitutional status to co‑operatives; don't confuse.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Gandhian Directives (Art 40, 43 part, 46, 47, 48)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 40: Organisation of village panchayats as units of self‑government.\nLed to the 73rd CAA 1992 (Part IX).\nTRAP: The directive existed since 1950; constitutional status came only in 1992.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 46: Promotion of educational and economic interests of SCs, STs and other weaker sections; protect from social injustice and exploitation.\nUsed to justify reservation policies.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 47: Raise level of nutrition, standard of living, and public health; prohibition of consumption of intoxicating drinks and drugs that are injurious to health.\nTRAP: It's a directive for prohibition, not a fundamental right to prohibition.\nStates can regulate alcohol trade under Art 19(6) despite this directive.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 48: Organisation of agriculture and animal husbandry on modern and scientific lines; prohibit slaughter of cows, calves and other milch and draught cattle.\nTRAP: This is a Gandhian directive, not a fundamental right.\nSupreme Court has upheld state laws banning cow slaughter to a reasonable extent, balancing with Art 19(1)(g).",
                "kind": "article"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Liberal‑Intellectual Directives (Art 44, 45, 48A, 49, 50, 51)",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Art 44: Uniform Civil Code for all citizens.\nTRAP: A directive, not enforceable.\nGoa has a Portuguese‑origin common civil code, but it was NOT enacted under Art 44.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 45: Provision for early childhood care and education for children until they complete the age of six years [amended by 86th CAA 2002].\nTRAP: Earlier it covered free & compulsory education for 6‑14; that is now Art 21A.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 48A: Protection and improvement of environment, forests, and wildlife [42nd CAA].\nBasis for Environment Protection Act 1986, Wildlife Protection Act 1972, and judicial doctrines like 'sustainable development', 'precautionary principle', 'polluter pays'.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 49: Protect monuments, places and objects of artistic or historic interest of national importance.\nGave rise to the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act 1958.",
                "kind": "article"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 50: Separation of the judiciary from the executive in the public services of the State.\nLed to the separation of magistracy from executive control (Code of Criminal Procedure amendments).\nTRAP: This is a directive, not a fundamental right.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Art 51: Promote international peace and security, maintain just and honourable relations, foster respect for international law and treaty obligations, encourage settlement of international disputes by arbitration.\nTRAP: This is why India follows a policy of 'non‑intervention' and supports UN peacekeeping.",
                "kind": "article"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Directives Outside Part IV (High‑Yield TRAP Zone)",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Art 335 (Part XVI): Claims of SCs and STs to services and posts shall be consistent with maintenance of efficiency of administration.\nBalances reservation with merit.\nIn M.\nNagaraj v.\nUOI (2006), SC held that Art 335 is relevant while deciding validity of reservation in promotion.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 350A (Part XVII): Facilities for instruction in mother‑tongue at the primary stage to children belonging to linguistic minority groups.\nTRAP: It is a directive, not a fundamental right (but right to conserve language under Art 29 is an FR).",
            "kind": "article"
          },
          {
            "label": "Art 351 (Part XVII): Directive to the Union to promote the spread and development of Hindi language to serve as a medium of expression for the composite culture of India.\nTRAP: This does NOT give a fundamental right to impose Hindi; it's a directive.",
            "kind": "article"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "FR vs DPSP Conflict – Evolution & Current Position",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Champakam Dorairajan v.\nState of Madras (1951): FRs strictly prevail over DPSPs; DPSPs cannot override FRs.\nLed to 1st CAA.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Golaknath v.\nState of Punjab (1967): Parliament cannot amend FRs to implement DPSPs.\nFRs are transcendent and immutable.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "25th CAA 1971: Inserted Art 31C – any law giving effect to Art 39(b) or (c) cannot be challenged under Art 14 or 19.\n(This was to overcome Golaknath and give primacy to certain DPSPs.)",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Kesavananda Bharati v.\nState of Kerala (1973): Upheld the first part of Art 31C (primacy of 39(b)&(c) over Art 14/19) but struck down the part that ousted judicial review.\nHeld that Parliament cannot destroy the basic structure.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "42nd CAA 1976: Expanded Art 31C to all DPSPs – every law implementing any DPSP was immune from Art 14 & 19 challenge.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Minerva Mills Ltd.\nv.\nUnion of India (1980): Struck down the 42nd CAA expansion of Art 31C.\nHeld that 'harmony and balance between Part III (FRs) and Part IV (DPSPs) is a basic feature of the Constitution'.\nResult: Art 31C protection limited ONLY to laws implementing Art 39(b)&(c).",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Waman Rao v.\nUnion of India (1981): Upheld the constitutionality of the first part of Art 31C (protection for 39(b)&(c) against Art 14/19).\nClarified that amendments to the 9th Schedule after Kesavananda (24 April 1973) are open to basic structure review.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "I.R.\nCoelho v.\nState of Tamil Nadu (2007): Reiterated that laws placed in 9th Schedule after 24 April 1973 cannot have blanket immunity; can be tested against FRs forming part of basic structure.\nIndirectly reinforces that DPSP laws (if placed in 9th Schedule) still must respect basic structure.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "CURRENT POSITION:\n\n1.\nFundamental Rights are generally supreme; DPSPs cannot override them.\n2.\nException - Under Art 31C, laws implementing Art 39(b) and 39(c) get immunity from Art 14 and Art 19.\n3.\nHarmony between Part III and Part IV is part of the Basic Structure; courts interpret FRs in light of DPSPs and vice versa.\n4.\nOther DPSPs can still be implemented, provided they do not violate Fundamental Rights.",
            "kind": "memory"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "TRAP Compendium for Prelims (Quick Recall)",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Are DPSPs justiciable? No.\nBut they are fundamental in governance and aid judicial review.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which DPSPs were added by 42nd CAA? 39(f), 39A, 43A, 48A (mnemonic: FEAL – 39(f), 39A, 43A, 48A (Environment) – actually 48A, not F).",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which DPSP was added by 44th CAA? Art 38(2) – minimising inequalities.",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which article is the source of the 'right to work'? Art 41 (directive, not FR).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Gandhian DPSP not listed in academic classification? Art 47 (prohibition) and Art 48 (cow slaughter) are Gandhian; Art 40 (panchayats) is Gandhian.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "DPSP outside Part IV? Art 335, 350A, 351.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which DPSP gives primacy over Art 14 & 19? Only those under Art 39(b) & (c), through Art 31C.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Is Uniform Civil Code a fundamental right? No, it is a directive under Art 44.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Difference between Art 21A and Art 45: 21A = FR for 6‑14; 45 = DPSP for 0‑6.",
            "kind": "comparison"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "VIII",
    "p": "pm2",
    "label": "Fundamental Duties (Art 51A) – Air‑1 Complete",
    "kind": "section",
    "children": [
      {
        "label": "Origin & Evolution",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Swaran Singh Committee (1976) Recommendations",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "Recommended 8 Fundamental Duties; Parliament accepted 10 via the 42nd CAA.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "Rejected recommendations: (1) Penalty for non‑compliance with duties, (2) Inclusion of duty to pay taxes.\nThese were considered impractical and contrary to voluntary spirit.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              },
              {
                "label": "Committee also suggested that no court could question whether a law implementing a duty violated FRs – this was also dropped.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Constitutional Incorporation",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "42nd CAA 1976: Inserted Part IV‑A (Art 51A) with 10 duties.\nInspired by USSR Constitution.",
                "kind": "amendment"
              },
              {
                "label": "86th CAA 2002: Added 11th duty – duty of parent/guardian to provide opportunities for education to child aged 6‑14 years (linked to Art 21A).",
                "kind": "amendment"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Key Features & Nature",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Non‑justiciable: Cannot be enforced by courts; yet fundamental in governance, similar to DPSPs.\nTRAP: Parliament can enact specific laws to enforce duties, and courts may use duties to interpret statutes and uphold constitutionality of laws (AIIMS Students Union v.\nAIIMS, 2001).",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Applicable ONLY to citizens (not foreigners).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Inspired by: USSR Constitution; also influenced by the Japanese and Weimar Constitutions.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: Fundamental Duties are NOT applicable to corporations or legal persons – only to natural persons who are citizens.",
            "kind": "trap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Complete List of 11 Duties with Nuances & Traps",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "51A(a) – To abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Failure to stand during the National Anthem may be violative of Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971, and also breach of duty (Bijoe Emmanuel case – Jehovah's Witnesses allowed to not sing but must stand and show respect).",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This duty does not criminalise peaceful criticism of the Constitution.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(b) – To cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle for freedom",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "No specific penal law; moral obligation only.",
                "kind": "bullet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(c) – To uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Overlaps with provisions of sedition (Sec 124A IPC) and anti‑terror laws.\nBut mere expression of dissent is not violation.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(d) – To defend the country and render national service when called upon to do so",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Conscription is NOT a constitutional mandate, but duty implies moral obligation.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(e) – To promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all the people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional or sectional diversities; to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This duty was cited in Shayara Bano (triple talaq) judgment to justify the ban on instant triple talaq as it derogated dignity of women.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(f) – To value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: 'Composite culture' is a deliberate phrase rejecting the theory of two‑nation divide; includes all religious and cultural streams.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(g) – To protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Supreme Court in M.C.\nMehta v.\nKamal Nath (2000) and Animal Welfare Board v.\nA.\nNagaraja (2014) read this duty with Art 21 to expand environmental and animal rights jurisprudence.",
                "kind": "trap"
              },
              {
                "label": "TRAP: The duty of compassion to living creatures is the basis for cow protection laws upheld by courts under Art 48 (DPSP).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(h) – To develop the scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: SC in Society for Enlightenment and Voluntary Action v.\nUnion of India (2016) held that propagating superstition is antithetical to this duty.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(i) – To safeguard public property and to abjure violence",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: Bandhs and hartals that involve destruction of public property have been held violative of this duty (Destruction of Public Property case, SC guidelines 2007).",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(j) – To strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity so that the nation constantly rises to higher levels of endeavour and achievement",
            "kind": "subsection",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This duty is often cited in education‑related PILs to promote quality and discourage negligence.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "51A(k) – [Added by 86th CAA 2002] Who is a parent or guardian to provide opportunities for education to his child or, as the case may be, ward between the age of six and fourteen years",
            "kind": "case",
            "children": [
              {
                "label": "TRAP: This is the ONLY duty that explicitly casts a specific obligation on parents/guardians.\nLinked with Art 21A (FR).\nNon‑compliance does not give rise to a private cause of action but influences RTE Act interpretation.",
                "kind": "trap"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Verma Committee (1999) & Legal Enforcement",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Committee on Operationalisation of Fundamental Duties (Chair: Justice J.S.\nVerma) recommended strategies for implementing duties.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Identified existing laws that indirectly enforce duties: Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act 1971 (duty a), Protection of Civil Rights Act 1955 (duty e), Wildlife Protection Act 1972 & Forest Conservation Act 1980 (duty g), Representation of the People Act 1951 (various duties).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: While duties are non‑justiciable, Parliament CAN enact penal laws to enforce them, provided they do not infringe FRs.",
            "kind": "trap"
          },
          {
            "label": "Verma Committee also recommended that duties be promoted through education, media, and public campaigns, and suggested incorporating them into the curriculum.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Judicial Interpretation & Linkages",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "AIIMS Students Union v.\nAIIMS (2001): SC held that FDs, though not enforceable by writ, provide a valuable parameter for construing statutes and judging the reasonableness of restrictions under Art 19.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "M.C.\nMehta (Taj Trapezium case, 1996): Used Art 51A(g) to direct industries to switch to clean fuel, holding that protecting environment is a fundamental duty of every citizen.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Shayara Bano v.\nUnion of India (2017): Justice Kurian Joseph invoked Art 51A(e) to declare triple talaq unconstitutional, stating that the practice is derogatory to the dignity of women.",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "Union of India v.\nNaveen Jindal (2004): Observed that respect for the National Flag is a fundamental duty, and the right to fly the national flag with dignity is a part of Art 19(1)(a), balanced by the duty under Art 51A(a).",
            "kind": "case"
          },
          {
            "label": "TRAP: FDs are often used as 'interpretative lenses' by courts to resolve conflicts between FRs and state action.",
            "kind": "trap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "High‑Yield Traps & Prelims Pearls",
        "kind": "subsection",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "Are Fundamental Duties justiciable? NO.\nBut they supplement FRs and DPSPs and are fundamental in governance.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "How many duties originally? 10 (1976).\nHow many now? 11 (2002).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which duty was added by 86th CAA? Duty of parent/guardian to educate child 6‑14 (51A(k)).",
            "kind": "amendment"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which committee recommended FDs? Swaran Singh Committee (1976).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "What did Swaran Singh recommend that was rejected? Penalty for non‑compliance and duty to pay taxes.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which article contains FDs? Art 51A (Part IV‑A).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Do FDs apply to foreigners? No, only to citizens.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Is paying taxes a Fundamental Duty? NO.\nVote? NO.\nFamily planning? NO.\nRespect parents? NO.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Name any legislation enforcing FDs: Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act 1971, Wildlife Protection Act 1972, etc.",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which duty is related to environment? Art 51A(g).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "Which duty is about composite culture? Art 51A(f).",
            "kind": "bullet"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Mnemonic for the 11 Duties (Air‑1 Quick Recall)",
        "kind": "memory",
        "children": [
          {
            "label": "A – Abide by Constitution/Flag/Anthem",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "B – Cherish noble ideals of freedom struggle",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "C – Uphold sovereignty, unity, integrity",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "D – Defend country, render national service",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "E – Promote harmony, renounce practices derogatory to women",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "F – Preserve composite culture",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "G – Protect environment (Green)",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "H – Scientific temper, humanism, inquiry",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "I – Safeguard public property, abjure violence",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "J – Strive for excellence",
            "kind": "bullet"
          },
          {
            "label": "K – Parent/guardian to educate child (Kids)",
            "kind": "bullet"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
