      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "B",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-005",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding the loss of Indian citizenship: 1. When an Indian citizen voluntarily acquires the citizenship of another country, his Indian citizenship automatically terminates. 2. The Central Government can legally deprive a person of their Indian citizenship if they have shown disloyalty to the Constitution. 3. A person who acquired Indian citizenship by birth can be deprived of it by the Central Government on grounds of fraud. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "B",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-006",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding the eligibility and limitations of Overseas Citizens of India (OCI): 1. A foreign national whose parents or grandparents were citizens of Pakistan or Bangladesh is permanently ineligible for OCI status. 2. An OCI cardholder is constitutionally eligible for appointment as a Judge of the Supreme Court of India. 3. Under Article 16, an OCI cardholder is eligible to hold public services and posts in connection with the affairs of the Union. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "A",
      "difficulty": "mid"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-007",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding dual citizenship and constitutional provisions: 1. Article 9 of the Constitution strictly prohibits dual citizenship by stating that no person shall be a citizen of India if they voluntarily acquired the citizenship of a foreign state. 2. The Supreme Court of India recognizes the OCI scheme introduced in 2005 as a legal form of dual citizenship. 3. An Indian citizen can hold dual citizenship if the second citizenship is of a country that offers reciprocal dual citizenship to Indians. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "A",
      "difficulty": "mid"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-008",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding the acquisition of citizenship by Naturalization and Registration: 1. A person of Indian origin who is ordinarily resident in India for seven years can acquire citizenship by Naturalization. 2. A foreigner (not of Indian origin) who is married to an Indian citizen can acquire Indian citizenship by Naturalization. 3. The Central Government may waive all conditions for naturalization if the person has rendered distinguished service to the cause of science, philosophy, art, literature, world peace, or human progress. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "A",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-009",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding Citizenship by Descent: 1. Prior to 1992, a person born outside India was a citizen by descent only if their father was an Indian citizen at the time of birth. 2. The 1992 amendment granted citizenship by descent if either the father or the mother is a citizen of India at the time of birth. 3. Since December 2004, a person born outside India shall not be a citizen by descent unless their birth is registered at an Indian consulate within one year. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "C",
      "difficulty": "mid"
