    {
      "id": "citizen-final-010",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding the renunciation and termination of citizenship during a war in which India is engaged: 1. If an Indian citizen declares renunciation of their citizenship during such a war, the registration of the declaration shall be withheld by the Central Government. 2. The automatic termination of Indian citizenship (upon acquiring a foreign passport) is kept in abeyance during such a war until the Central Government otherwise directs. 3. When a person legally renounces their Indian citizenship, their minor children automatically retain their Indian citizenship unconditionally. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "B",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-011",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding Section 6A of the Citizenship Act, 1955 (related to the Assam Accord): 1. It grants immediate citizenship with voting rights to persons of Indian origin who came to Assam from Bangladesh between January 1, 1966, and March 25, 1971. 2. It mandates that those who entered Assam after March 25, 1971, are to be detected and deported in accordance with law. 3. The Supreme Court recently struck down Section 6A, declaring it violative of the fundamental right to equality (Article 14). How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "A",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
