      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding aliens in India: 1. Enemy aliens do not enjoy protection against arrest and detention under Article 22 of the Constitution. 2. Friendly aliens enjoy the right to equality before the law and equal protection of the laws under Article 14. 3. Children of foreign diplomats born in India automatically acquire Indian citizenship by birth due to the jus soli principle. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "B",
      "difficulty": "mid"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-013",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding the Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2019: 1. It reduces the residency requirement for naturalization from 11 years to 5 years for specified persecuted minorities. 2. It grants a pathway to citizenship for persecuted minorities from Pakistan, Bangladesh, Afghanistan, and Myanmar. 3. To be eligible, the individual must have entered India on or before December 31, 2014. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "B",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-014",
      "q": "Consider the following statements regarding Citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution (Articles 5-8): 1. Under Article 5, possessing a 'domicile' in the territory of India was an essential condition for acquiring citizenship. 2. A person who migrated to Pakistan from India after March 1, 1947, but returned to India for resettlement under a permit, could never become an Indian citizen. 3. The Constitution defines the term 'domicile' comprehensively under Part II. How many of the above statements are correct?",
      "options": { "A": "Only one", "B": "Only two", "C": "All three", "D": "None" },
      "answer": "A",
      "difficulty": "mid"
    },
    {
      "id": "citizen-final-015",
      "q": "The Central Government has the statutory power to cancel the registration of an Overseas Citizen of India (OCI) cardholder. Which of the following can act as valid grounds for such cancellation? 1. The OCI cardholder has shown disaffection towards the Constitution of India. 2. The OCI cardholder is sentenced to imprisonment for more than two years within five years of their registration. 3. It is necessary in the interest of the sovereignty and integrity of India. Select the correct answer:",
      "options": { "A": "1 and 2 only", "B": "2 and 3 only", "C": "1 and 3 only", "D": "1, 2, and 3" },
